fewer arithmetic cells do not guarantee faster FPGA timing.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (improvement, +11.34%): Parallel D/2D/3D trial subtraction with registered 3D and one-hot selection in `alu.sv` shortened each divider round from four dependent radix-2 steps to two radix-4 digits, improving fitness without increasing the nine-clock DIV/REM latency.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r4s0 (regression, +8.24%): Removing the IF instruction-to-NOP mux shortened the redirect-to-hazard control path and raised median Gowin Fmax to 50.514 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cyc\n…[4569 of 7641 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:360b997341bda0e4]…\n acceptance margin.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r10s1 (regression, -1.93%): Retiming limb products and divider completion across EX/MEM added 35,538 CoreMark cycles through late-result interlocks while register-file/branch-recovery feedback limited median Fmax to 55.621 MHz, so arithmetic pipelining must clear competing control paths to repay the new dependency bubbles.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r10s2 (regression, -5.79%): Holding a registered correction PC until fetch acceptance preserves the one-slot miss penalty and removes combinational EX recovery, but adds a full-width mux ahead of imem and prediction without relieving the remaining register-file/multiplier and divider-completion timing limits.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r11s0 (regression, -4.45%): Predecoding 17 result producers in ID/EX and using a balanced masked-OR tree left the register-file-to-EX/MEM datapath critical, so flattening result selection alone did not improve whole-core timing (53.736 MHz median) at unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
