{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r9s1.yaml; cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261003-002-r9s1.last'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261003-002-r9s1\ntitle: \"Predivide the leading nibble to isolate divider sign correction\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 133.79 at 56.239 MHz and 4,203,513 bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path starts in divider quotient\n  state and ends in EX/MEM after two radix-4 digits and sign correction.\n  The r8s1 result-banking attempt regressed by 4.90 percent and merely\n  moved the worst endpoint to the divider's own held-result register:\n  23.912 ns across 75 cells. This points to the final arithmetic chain,\n  rather than just its destination mux. Adding a completion clock would\n  violate the existing redirect-plus-divide progress bound. A specialized\n  leading-nibble step can free a clock for correction without increasing\n  the nine-clock execution latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Reschedule the blocking divider so the request-capture edge also resolves\n  the most significant four dividend bits, seven subsequent clocks each\n  compute the existing two radix-4 digits, and the ninth EX clock only\n  selects and sign-corrects a fully registered quotient or remainder.\n  This is one structural change to division scheduling and register\n  placement. Preserve the ordinary ALU, multiplier, fetch prediction,\n  register file, forwarding topology, independent effective-address\n  register, pipeline depth and all instruction acceptance/retirement cycles.\n\n  Let A and D be the existing 32-bit unsigned operand magnitudes and let\n  N=A[31:28]. At request edge 0, compute the exact quotient nibble q0 and\n  remainder r0 for N divided by D. If any D[31:4] bit is set, D>=16>N,\n  hence q0=0 and r0=N. Otherwise use a small, purely combinational 4-bit\n  unsigned divider for N and D[3:0]. Implement it as four width-bounded\n  restoring bit steps with five-bit trial/subtraction temporaries, not a\n  32-bit divider, large variable shift or inferred / or % operator. D=0\n  naturally produces q0=15 and r0=N. Start the existing recurrence with\n  quotient_q={A[27:0],q0} and remainder_q=zero_extend(r0). Capture D,\n…[5144 of 8216 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d8bd774864a5844e]…\n,\n      test the full-width divisor bypass and arithmetic corner cases, and\n      verify stable completion under held consume and changing live inputs.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing architectural and exact progress assertions. Cover\n      redirect-to-divide, consecutive dependent divisions, high divisors,\n      zero divisors, signed corner cases and memory stalls surrounding\n      request/completion; require unchanged RVFI results, operands, order,\n      once-only retirement and the ten-clock ready-bus progress bound.\nWrote and schema-validated [hyp-20261003-002-r9s1.yaml](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-astra_xhigh-v2-rep6/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261003-002-r9s1-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r9s1.yaml).\n\nProposes resolving four division bits during operand capture to isolate final sign correction while preserving nine-clock latency. Expected fitness gain: **10%**, unmeasured. Arithmetic-model checks passed; RTL and FPGA validation remain.", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
