ess without increasing the nine-clock DIV/REM latency.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r4s0 (regression, +8.24%): Removing the IF instruction-to-NOP mux shortened the redirect-to-hazard control path and raised median Gowin Fmax to 50.514 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles; accepted invalid fetches must clear the entire ID/EX payload because downstream controls are not uniformly gated by valid, while stalls must preserve held instructions.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r4s2 (improvement, +8.92%): Learning agreement with the branch displacement sign lets oppositely biased branches reinforce shared counters, enabling a 16-entry table with local updates and an MSB-only lookup plus direct-JAL prediction to deliver a net CoreMark fitness gain.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r5s0 (improvement, +7.50%): A separate EX/MEM register for full forwarded `rs1 + imm`, reset to zero and held only by dmem stall, bypasses the general ALU/M-extension result mux on the memory-address path and improves Fmax without changing CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r5s1 (regression, +5.85%): Registering ALU/divider/link results before their shared EX/MEM selection mux shortened arithmetic paths and shifted the bottleneck to branch recovery/register-file control, but preserving Gowin BSRAM inference required a separately registered effective address that bypasses the post-register mux.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
