hout increasing the nine-clock DIV/REM latency.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r4s0 (regression, +8.24%): Removing the IF instruction-to-NOP mux shortened the redirect-to-hazard control path and raised median Gowin Fmax to 50.514 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cyc\n…[7423 of 10495 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:71b61d2e41a0b845]…\npost-selection negation carry chain, improving Fmax without changing CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-002-r13s1 (regression, +16.26%): A two-bit request head start removes the final radix-4 digit from `m_finish.sv` at unchanged CoreMark cycles, but exposes `alu.sv`'s `divisor_q`-to-`remainder_q` recurrence as the critical path, leaving median Fmax at 79.675 MHz versus 81.140 MHz for parallel signed-candidate completion.\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-003-r13s2 (regression, +16.40%): A 16-entry tagged BTB trades IF's instruction-dependent target adder for cold/conflict recoveries (33,452 extra CoreMark cycles in the prototype), so its timing gain still lost to the cycle-neutral divider optimization (188.18 versus 191.41 fitness).\n- 2026-10-04 hyp-20261004-001-r14s0 (improvement, +5.91%): Parallel radix-16 trials in `alu.sv` plus complete unsigned EX/MEM results shorten both the serial radix-4 recurrence and the `m_finish.sv` completion path, improving timing while preserving nine-clock DIV/REM occupancy.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
