{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_40", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r4s0.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261003-001-r4s0\ntitle: \\\"Map the small integer register file to explicit flop words and parallel reads\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 103.91 at 46.673 MHz. A fresh Gowin placement-0\n  run reproduces 46.361 MHz and its 26.865 ns worst path. The detailed\n  report identifies a register-file BSRAM output, two ID operand mux\n  cells, EX forwarding/source selection, the unsigned multiplier DSP,\n  and the memory address endpoint. A nearly tied 26.864 ns branch and\n  hazard-control path also starts at a register-file BSRAM output.\n  The BSRAM clock-to-output delay is 2.260 ns. Replacing the inferred\n  small-memory mapping can improve both paths at their shared source.\n  Earlier micro_opt attempts at combinational division and custom\n  borrow prefixes lost timing, so another arithmetic prefix is not\n  supported by the evidence.\nhypothesis: |\n  Change only the physical RTL organization of the 32-by-32 integer\n  register file: implement x1 through x31 as separately declared\n  scalar 32-bit flop words with constant-index write enables, and\n  implement the two asynchronous reads as shared address decoding\n  followed by balanced masked-OR trees. x0 remains a literal zero.\n  This is a local register-file mapping optimization, with the same\n  two combinational read ports, synchronous write, synchronous reset,\n  same-cycle write-first bypass, and unchanged pipeline boundaries.\n\n  Use a generate scope for each architectural register 1 through 31.\n  Declare one scalar word_q in each scope, reset it to zero, and write\n  it only when w_en && w_addr equals that scope's constant register\n  index. Avoid an unpacked storage array with dynamic read or write\n  indexing, which currently permits Gowin to infer block RAM and\n  absorb operand-register behavior into its synchronous output path.\n  Decode rs1_addr and rs2_addr once per word; gate each word with its\n  read select and combine the 32 leaves, including a zero x0 leaf,\n  in a balanced binary OR tree for each port. Give all constants and\n  comparisons explicit widths compatible with Gowin and Yosys.\n\n  Preserve write-first behavior with a final bypass when w_en is set,\n  w_addr is nonzero, and the requested read address equals w_addr.\n  Reading x0 must always produce zero, including a simultaneous\n  attempted write to x0. Every nonzero register must still read zero\n  after reset; do not remove initialization or weaken the existing\n  reset-clears-all test to encourage RAM inference. Preserve exact\n  hold behavior when w_en is zero, and allow independent read addresses\n  with at most one write per edge. Use portable RTL, not vendor-only\n  memory primitives, timing exceptions, or changes to FPGA constraints.\n\n  Keep the EX forwarding logic, ALU, radix-4 divider, decoder, and\n  pipeline control unchanged. This mapping change must not add a cycle\n  to reads, writes, loads, multiplies, or division. Preserve all bus\n  handshakes, trapped instruction behavior, nret=1 ports, RVFI source\n  values, and exactly-once ordered retirement. The gain should come\n  from replacing slow inferred BSRAM outputs and associated operand\n  mux logic with ordinary flop-to-flop paths, not from pruning RVFI.\n\n  Expect approximately 15 percent higher fitness with unchanged\n  bracketed CoreMark iterations/cycle. Verify the mapped netlist has\n  flop-based register storage and measure the complete timed core.\n  Acceptance requires median Gowin Fmax times measured iterations/cycle\n  above 108.68986. At the historical 4,491,485 cycles for ten iterations,\n  the frequency threshold is approximately 48.82 MHz. Confirm cycles,\n  canonical CoreMark CRCs, validation text, normal formal checks, and\n  existing stalled pipeline behavior. This spends roughly one thousand\n  register bits and read-selection LUTs to shorten timing; risks are\n  read-tree routing and fanout, additional area affecting placement,\n  and the unchanged divider becoming limiting before the required gain.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 15\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the dynamically indexed storage array with 31 scalar\n      generated flop words, per-word synchronous write enables, shared\n      constant-address read decodes, and balanced masked-OR read trees.\n      Keep the existing module ports, reset-to-zero semantics, x0,\n      combinational read latency, and same-cycle write-first bypass.\n      Preserve every other RTL module and all pipeline registers.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Preserve all existing reset, read, write, x0, and bypass checks.\n      Extend coverage to every nonzero destination and independently\n      selected read ports, disabled writes, both ports bypassing the\n      same write, one port bypassing while the other reads storage,\n      writes to x0, and reset after populating every register. Compare\n      deterministic randomized read/write sequences to a 32-word model\n      with synchronous reset and combinational write-first semantics.\n''')\nprint('Overwrote hypothesis with the single register-file mapping angle.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
