{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_88", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r6s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-001-r6s0',\n'title':'Keep register-file reads asynchronous and independent of recovery',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 121.67 at 51.146 MHz and 4,203,513 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its register file is mapped to block RAM with operand registers absorbed into the memory implementation. Detailed Gowin reports show nearly tied multiplier and branch-recovery paths starting at register-file block RAM; recovery also feeds the fetched-instruction NOP mux and returns through register-file read addressing. The prior micro_opt hyp-20261003-001-r4s0 removed that masking globally and gained on its older parent, but a fresh whole-payload-squash probe on this champion regressed to 47.842 MHz. Target the register-file read implementation directly while retaining the established instruction-validity and decode protocol.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Implement the existing 32x32, two-read/one-write integer register file as asynchronous distributed RAM, using Gowin's supported syn_ramstyle = \\\"distributed_ram\\\" attribute on regs. Keep the ID/EX operand registers as the existing pipeline boundary. Preserve the exact register-file behavioral RTL: synchronous reset clears all architectural registers, x0 always reads zero and ignores writes, and a matching same-cycle WB write bypasses the stored value. Do not introduce vendor primitives, clocks, pipeline stages, latency, or any change to architectural reset semantics.\n\nFor this asynchronous read implementation, drive only u_rf.rs1_addr and u_rf.rs2_addr directly from io_imemData[19:15] and [24:20]. Disconnect the now-unused id_stage read-address outputs and remove their top-level wires. Keep IF's instruction-to-NOP mux, IF/ID validity, decoder inputs, ID/EX capture and flush priorities, raw load-use comparisons, prediction, branch training, and all EX forwarding unchanged. An asynchronous RAM must not sit after a late recovery-dependent address mux: the direct addresses are integral to this mapping change. The attribute alone measured only 42.786 MHz, while direct addresses alone measured 50.962 MHz.\n\nEvery accepted valid instruction has identical raw and masked source fields. On a killed or unready fetch, arbitrary register-read values are harmless because the existing invalid-instruction/bubble protocol suppresses execution and retirement; retain that protocol verbatim. During dmem or divider holds, ID/EX retains its old operands regardless of changing raw fetch addresses. Preserve the current write-first bypass, held MEM/WB forwarding information, independent effective-address register and dmem hold behavior, arithmetic and ALTOPS schedules, trap checks, and nret=1 interface and RVFI order. No benchmark, wrapper, timing constraints, or decoder legality changes are needed.\n\nA proposal-stage implementation measured 53.023, 53.438, and 53.438 MHz for Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2. Its median is 53.438 MHz versus the reproduced parent median of 51.146 MHz. It uses 3,335 logic cells, 668 registers, 40 LUT-RAMs, four BSRAMs and six DSPs, versus 3,080/707/eight/six/six for the parent. The worst paths now cross forwarding/multiplication or divider completion rather than register-file recovery feedback. All 53 unchanged formal checks, register-file and pipeline unit runners, and cosimulation of five ELFs passed. The canonical stalled CoreMark run passed CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation and retained exactly 4,203,513 bracketed cycles.\n\nThis prototype gives about 4.48 percent higher fitness, just below the required 4.6 percent acceptance margin. The integer expectation is 5 percent, with medium confidence; no tournament acceptance is claimed. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 127.26682, or median Fmax above 53.4968 MHz at unchanged cycles. Remaining arithmetic timing and routing sensitivity are the main risks. Reproduce the final median and actual benchmark cycles, and preserve every unchanged correctness gate. The implementation should remain one register-file read-path optimization, without unrelated predictor or arithmetic changes.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':5,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'Apply the supported syn_ramstyle = \\\"distributed_ram\\\" attribute to the existing regs array. Preserve the synchronous reset loop, write enable and x0 protection, both combinational read ports, and same-cycle write-first bypass exactly. Confirm distributed-memory inference and that all FPGA memory resources fit.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Connect u_rf read addresses directly to raw io_imemData source fields, remove the obsolete rs1_addr_w/rs2_addr_w wires, and explicitly disconnect the now-unused u_id address outputs. Preserve masked instruction delivery and every existing hazard, prediction, hold, flush, memory, and RVFI connection.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Exercise arbitrary raw source fields on killed/unready fetches, wrong-path memory/divide/illegal words, raw-source load-use aliases, and simultaneous imem/dmem/divider holds. Verify held operands, valid retirement records, exact RVFI order and memory effects, and unchanged cycle timing. Preserve all existing assertions and use existing register-file reset/x0/write-first tests unchanged.'}\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=86))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Updated and schema-validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
