reMark.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.01%): A 128-entry bimodal predictor plus direct-JAL prediction cut CoreMark cycles by 6.4% by removing redirect bubbles, but the unchanged forwarding/combinational-ALU critical path kept Fmax near 5.45 MHz, limiting the fitness gain over baseline to 7%.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (improvement, +30.42%): Halving the divider's restoring recurrence from eight ste\n…[3593 of 6665 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6d0dd9ae4843dcb9]…\n.85%): A registered `kill_ex` with raw fetch data and control-only EX/MEM annulment preserved the single-bubble miss penalty, but the remaining register-file/ID/ALU-to-EX/MEM path limited median Fmax to 52.089 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles, leaving the gain below the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r7s1 (regression, -13.88%): Replacing inferred RAM with 992 flops and balanced masked-OR reads exposed recovery-dependent register addresses as the critical path into ID/EX, lowering median Fmax to 48.432 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles; balancing the read tree does not shorten its upstream WB/EX/IF control path.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r7s2 (regression, -4.34%): Preselecting `predicted_taken ? 4 : imm` before the EX recovery adder removes late branch resolution from target selection without addressing divider/multiplier timing limits or reducing CoreMark cycles, so shorter logical recovery feedback does not guarantee faster Gowin timing (median Fmax fell from 56.239 to 53.799 MHz).\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
