.fpga . $(TARGET)\n\ntiming:\n# Python\n__pycache__/\n*.pyc\n*.pyo\n.pytest_cache/\n.mypy_cache/\n\n# Toolchain (downloaded by setup.sh)\n.toolchain/\n\n# Vendored as submodule (initialized in Phase 4). No trailing slash so the\n# pattern matches both the cloned directory in the main repo AND the\n# symlink that worktree.\n…[4687 of 7759 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:04da51447cec4b0d]…\npshot: PATH + resolved EDA tool paths)\n# IS tracked, decided at the first v2 rep publish (e1a static, 2026-07-16):\n# it is tiny, contains no secrets, and pins which toolchain binaries\n# produced each rep's numbers.\n\n# Throwaway harness-verification runs (one-model YAMLs + their output\n# trees). These are reliability checks, not benchmark data — output is\n# regenerable and the YAMLs only encode \"rerun this single model to\n# confirm a fix landed\". Naming convention: `*-verify` for output dirs,\n# `models-*-verify.yaml` for the throwaway model YAML.\nbench/*-verify/\nbench/smoke-*/\ntools/bench/models-*-verify.yaml\n\n# Aborted/partial bench output preserved locally with a debug-tag suffix\n# (e.g. `gpt-5_5.failed-pwd-bug/`, `gpt-5_5.validate-v3/`, sibling\n# `results.jsonl.<tag>`). They document a known-broken state for the\n# author's own postmortem; the canonical results live in the un-suffixed\n# per-model dirs.\nbench/*.failed-*\nbench/*.validate-*\nbench/*.prev-*\nbench/*.stale-*\nbench/*.partial-*\nbench/results.jsonl.*\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
