{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"agent_message","text":"The parallel divider also missed the target; its worst path moved to register-file reads through multiplication. The final proposal will refine the strongest measured lead—distributed register-file reads—by using native 16-word banks and combining bank selection with writeback bypass. That refinement is unmeasured, and I’ll mark the confidence low.\n\n"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_59", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport shutil\nbase=Path('cores/bench'); save=base/'generated/r9s0_probe'\nfor name in ['alu.sv','core.sv','core_pkg.sv','ex_stage.sv','id_stage.sv','if_stage.sv','reg_file.sv','mem_stage.sv','wb_stage.sv']:\n shutil.copyfile(save/name,base/'rtl'/name)\nshutil.copyfile(save/'test_pipeline.py',base/'test/test_pipeline.py')\np=base/'experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-001-r9s0.yaml'\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261003-001-r9s0\ntitle: \\\"Merge register-file bank selection and writeback bypass\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 133.79 at 56.239 MHz with 4,203,513 bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. Recent micro_opt attempts on divider completion and\n  multiplier sharing exposed competing register-file-to-multiply and\n  recovery-to-register-file paths. The r6s0 distributed-RAM experiment\n  nearly cleared acceptance before forwarding was retimed, whereas\n  r7s1's flop register file regressed with recovery-dependent addresses.\n  Target the register-file read selection itself, preserving native\n  distributed storage and removing serial bank and writeback selection.\nhypothesis: |\n  Implement the existing 32-word register file as two explicit 16x32\n  distributed-RAM banks, covering x0..x15 and x16..x31. For each read\n  port, read both banks with address[3:0] and select among low-bank\n  data, high-bank data and same-cycle WB data using three mutually\n  exclusive masks and a parallel masked-OR. This is one local storage\n  mapping and read-mux optimization. Preserve both asynchronous reads,\n  one synchronous write, the existing ID/EX boundary and all latency.\n\n  Mark both arrays with Gowin's supported\n  syn_ramstyle = \\\"distributed_ram\\\" attribute. Split writes using\n  w_addr[4] and index the selected bank with w_addr[3:0]. Preserve the\n  existing synchronous reset semantics by clearing every bank word on\n  reset, and retain the write guard w_en && w_addr\"'!=0 with reset taking\n  priority. Do not substitute uninitialized data or a new reset-valid\n  bitmap for the existing architectural zero values. Keep x0 hardwired\n  to zero on reads and discard writes to it.\n\n  For each source address r, compute nz=(r!=0) and\n  hit=w_en && (w_addr==r). Define select_wb=nz && hit,\n  select_lo=nz && !hit && !r[4], and\n  select_hi=nz && !hit && r[4]. Produce the read value as\n  ({32{select_wb}} & w_data) OR\n  ({32{select_lo}} & bank_lo[r[3:0]]) OR\n  ({32{select_hi}} & bank_hi[r[3:0]]).\n  Implement the masks and parallel reduction explicitly; do not build\n  a bank-output mux followed by another writeback mux and x0 mux.\n  For x0 all masks are zero; otherwise exactly one is set. Matching\n  same-cycle writes must win even before the array'\"'s write edge,\n  exactly as in the original write-first interface. Preserve its\n  behavior during reset as well as after reset.\n\n  Drive only the register-file read addresses directly from raw\n  io_imemData[19:15] and io_imemData[24:20], disconnecting ID's now-unused\n  address outputs and removing obsolete top-level wires. This prevents\n  branch recovery from changing the distributed-RAM address through\n  IF's instruction-to-NOP mux. Keep that instruction mux and the entire\n  existing invalid-instruction protocol in place. Valid accepted words\n  have identical raw and masked source addresses. Invalid fetches may\n  read arbitrary registers but cannot commit those values; held ID/EX\n  operands remain unchanged during dmem or divider stalls.\n\n  Native 16-word banks match the FPGA's distributed-memory granularity.\n  Moving the upper-address decision into the same parallel selection\n  as WB bypass is intended to remove a serial mux from RAM data to the\n  existing operand registers. It should also shorten the portion of\n  recovery feedback that currently enters register-file addressing.\n  Confirm actual distributed-memory inference and resource use in\n  Gowin; behavioral array dimensions alone do not establish mapping.\n  Keep both banks as memories, not 992 individually decoded data flops.\n\n  Preserve every other pipeline control and data path: binary forwarding\n  selectors captured in ID/EX, trap-aware producer eligibility, retained\n  MEM/WB forwarding after retirement, raw load-use comparisons, effective\n  addresses, bus handshakes, all ALU and real/ALTOPS divider behavior,\n  predictor policy, decoder legality, nret=1 and exact RVFI source data\n  and contiguous order. Do not qualify load-use by architectural source\n  usage: the unchanged full-trace checks also observe unused fields.\n\n  During proposal research, a simpler distributed-RAM attribute plus\n  raw-address variant on this champion measured median 55.321 MHz and\n  passed the register-file and pipeline runners. It did not improve\n  fitness or clear acceptance. The final two-bank, combined-selection\n  refinement described here has NOT been implemented or timed, so those\n  results are not evidence that this final proposal passes or wins.\n  The extra bank ports, control fanout, reset mapping and routing may\n  erase the intended benefit; divider completion remains a competing\n  limit. Expect about 6 percent higher fitness with low confidence.\n  Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 139.94434, approximately\n  58.826 MHz at unchanged cycles. Evaluate median Gowin Fmax together\n  with actual marker-bracketed iterations per cycle.\n\n  Validate reset after prior writes, x0, both banks and boundary x15/x16,\n  every read/write address pair, same-cycle bypass on either or both\n  ports, and independent simultaneous reads. Add pipeline cases with\n  arbitrary raw source fields on killed/unready fetches and overlapping\n  memory/divider holds. Preserve all existing architectural assertions,\n  formal/liveness checks, stalled RVFI cosimulation, canonical CoreMark\n  CRC/UART validation, memory bounds and MMIO timing brackets. Run the\n  unchanged arithmetic suites as part of the implementation gates.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Split the array into two native 16x32 distributed-RAM banks.\n      Preserve synchronous reset and write behavior. Merge bank selection,\n      x0 masking and write-first bypass into a parallel three-source read\n      reduction independently for both ports, with mutually exclusive masks.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect register-file reads to raw fetch source fields, remove the\n      unused address wires and explicitly disconnect ID address outputs.\n      Preserve masked instruction delivery, validity and all pipeline wiring.\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Cover both banks, all source/destination address pairs, x15/x16,\n      x0, reset after writes, and same-cycle write-first behavior on\n      either and both read ports using an independent register model.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise raw source fields during invalid fetches, recovery,\n      load-use, dmem holds and divider waits. Retain exact RVFI operands,\n      order, memory effects, forwarding and progress assertions.\n''')\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert h['id']=='hyp-20261003-001-r9s0' and h['category']=='micro_opt'\nassert type(h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nassert -50 <= h['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct'] <= 50\nprint('Final hypothesis written; schema validation passed.')\nPY\ngit status --short\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
