{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_22", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r11s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261003-002-r11s1\ntitle: \"Decouple fetch and decode with a two-entry instruction queue\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 4,203,513 bracketed CoreMark cycles at 56.239 MHz.\n  Its fused fetch/decode stage cannot retain an instruction independently\n  of ID/EX: load-use, data-memory and divider stalls hold the fetch PC,\n  while an instruction-bus stall injects a bubble even if earlier cycles\n  had spare fetch bandwidth. Prior structural changes repeatedly moved\n  the timing limit between divider completion, multiplication and\n  register-file/recovery feedback. In particular, r10s1 added 35,538\n  cycles without improving Fmax. A registered instruction queue instead\n  targets lost bus overlap and separates decode data from live fetch and\n  recovery control, without adding arithmetic dependency latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert a two-entry, fully registered, in-order instruction queue between\n  fetch and decode. This is a single structural change to the front-end\n  pipeline and its backpressure topology. Each entry contains PC, the\n  unmodified instruction and its fetch-time predicted_taken bit. Fetch\n  uses the existing sixteen-entry agree predictor and direct-JAL target\n  calculation. Advance the fetch PC only when a ready bus word is actually\n  enqueued, or on an accepted EX recovery. Decode consumes only the\n  registered head; there is no combinational empty-queue bypass.\n\n  Fetch may enqueue while ID/EX is held by load-use, a data-memory request\n  or a divide, until the queue fills. Decode may dequeue while imemReady\n  is low. Support simultaneous push/pop, including a full queue replacing\n  its consumed head, so steady-state throughput remains one instruction\n  per clock. Use two compacting entry registers with a registered count\n  or equivalent occupancy bits: the head is directly available without\n  a variable-index RAM read. For a simultaneous pop/push at occupancy one,\n  replace the head with the incoming entry; at occupancy two, move the\n  tail to the head and capture the new tai\n…[5410 of 8482 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5575f2024a47ad19]…\nentry boundary and retain the\n      PC, instruction, predicted-taken and valid bundle fields. Add only\n      internal queue types or handshake declarations needed for this change.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Test queue ordering, immutable prediction snapshots, reset, capacity,\n      simultaneous enqueue/dequeue, stable head during holds and redirect\n      priority over both bus availability and queue fullness. Check that\n      each accepted fetch appears exactly once unless explicitly squashed.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Extend the fetch/predictor scoreboard to distinguish enqueue from\n      decode acceptance. Cover buffered dependency chains, forwarding at\n      dequeue, bus-stall overlap, divider holds and wrong-path cancellation.\n      Retain exact architectural operands, results, traps, PC sequence,\n      retirement order and progress assertions; adapt only internal-stage\n      monitors and fixed startup timing to the explicit new fetch boundary.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
