{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwanted={'hyp-20261003-002-r8s1','hyp-20261003-002-r9s1','hyp-20261003-002-r4s1'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    d=json.loads(line)\n    if d['id'] in wanted:\n        print(d['id'],d['title'])\n        print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 Localize fetch squashing at the ID/EX control boundary\nRestructure the IF-to-ID bubble protocol so instruction data travels\nindependently of squash control. Represent a missing or killed fetch\nwith its valid bit, and make ID/EX control capture the point where\ninvalid work becomes inert. This is one structural change to the\nfront-end control topology: move the kill boundary from the wide\nfetch instruction bus to the registered decoded-control boundary.\nKeep the current pipeline depth, acceptance schedule, register file,\nforwarding, arithmetic units, and branch policy.\n\nIn if_stage.sv, drive out.instr directly from imem_data even during\nflush or redirect. Keep out.pc tied to the current PC and retain\nout.valid = !(flush || redirect). Preserve reset priority, redirect\npriority over stall, and ordinary PC advance only when not stalled.\nRemove the NOP-substitution mux from instruction and register-file\naddress paths. Do not recreate squash gating on these data paths.\n\nIn id_stage.sv, preserve reset/flush priority over hold and capture.\nWhen capture is allowed, assign reg_q.ctrl = in.valid ? ctrl_decoded\n: '0, and capture reg_q.valid = in.valid. Other payload fields retain\ntheir current capture behavior; their values are irrelevant when\nvalid is zero. A held ID/EX instruction retains all controls and\noperands, including a held divide or an instruction behind a stalled\nmemory operation. A redirect or load-use flush still clears ID/EX.\n\nClearing every invalid decoded control is essential. hazard_unit\nreads id_ex.ctrl.mem_read without qualifying it by id_ex.valid.\nMerely clearing valid would let raw stalled imem_data create a ghost\nload dependency after fetch resumes. EX must retain its existing\nfully inert EX/MEM bubble for invalid input because memory and\nforwarding consumers are not universally valid-qualified. Keep the\ndecoder's default-illegal policy and all checks on valid encodings;\nsanitizing an invalid fetch must not legalize a valid illegal opcode.\n\nKeep current hazard priorities. Raw sourc\n…[12280 of 15352 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:31013193c15ae6b5]…\nedirect to\ndivide. Run existing ALU/pipeline tests, unchanged formal and real stalled\nCoreMark retirement cosimulation with canonical CRC/UART/bounds/markers.\n\nA standalone integer model of this schedule passed all 256 small-divider\ninput pairs and 41,296 full-width DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU cases from directed\nboundaries and seeded-random operands, checking each registered recurrence\nagainst exact prefix division. This supports the arithmetic construction;\nit is not RTL verification, a formal pass or a timing measurement.\n\nExpected benefit is unchanged CoreMark cycles and shorter final-divider\ntiming. The leading-nibble work lengthens operand capture; magnitude\ngeneration followed by the small divider or the unchanged multiply and\nrecovery paths may become limiting. Check complete Gowin timing rather\nthan treating removal of the old endpoint as proof. Acceptance requires\nmeasured median Fmax times iterations/cycle above 139.94434, approximately\n58.826 MHz at 4,203,513 cycles. The expected gain below is unmeasured.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
