{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_53", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r4s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261003-002-r4s1\ntitle: \\\"Localize fetch squashing at the ID/EX control boundary\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 103.91 at 46.673 MHz with 4,491,485 bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. Prior structural changes shortened division, but\n  the timing limit now includes a front-end control loop. A local\n  make timing TARGET=bench reproduced placement-0 Fmax of 46.361 MHz.\n  Its worst path crosses the multiplier at 26.865 ns, while a nearly\n  identical 26.864 ns path crosses register-file read, forwarding,\n  branch resolution, IF instruction masking, load-use detection, and\n  the ID operand-register reset. Replacing a killed fetch with a NOP\n  sends a late redirect back through source-address and hazard logic.\n  Moving squash handling to the receiving pipeline boundary should\n  shorten this feedback without increasing instruction cycles.\nhypothesis: |\n  Restructure the IF-to-ID bubble protocol so instruction data travels\n  independently of squash control. Represent a missing or killed fetch\n  with its valid bit, and make ID/EX control capture the point where\n  invalid work becomes inert. This is one structural change to the\n  front-end control topology: move the kill boundary from the wide\n  fetch instruction bus to the registered decoded-control boundary.\n  Keep the current pipeline depth, acceptance schedule, register file,\n  forwarding, arithmetic units, and branch policy.\n\n  In if_stage.sv, drive out.instr directly from imem_data even during\n  flush or redirect. Keep out.pc tied to the current PC and retain\n  out.valid = \"'!(flush || redirect). Preserve reset priority, redirect\n  priority over stall, and ordinary PC advance only when not stalled.\n  Remove the NOP-substitution mux from instruction and register-file\n  address paths. Do not recreate squash gating on these data paths.\n\n  In id_stage.sv, preserve reset/flush priority over hold and capture.\n  When capture is allowed, assign reg_q.ctrl = in.valid ? ctrl_decoded\n  : '\"'0, and capture reg_q.valid = in.valid. Other payload fields retain\n  their current capture behavior; their values are irrelevant when\n  valid is zero. A held ID/EX instruction retains all controls and\n  operands, including a held divide or an instruction behind a stalled\n  memory operation. A redirect or load-use flush still clears ID/EX.\n\n  Clearing every invalid decoded control is essential. hazard_unit\n  reads id_ex.ctrl.mem_read without qualifying it by id_ex.valid.\n  Merely clearing valid would let raw stalled imem_data create a ghost\n  load dependency after fetch resumes. EX must retain its existing\n  fully inert EX/MEM bubble for invalid input because memory and\n  forwarding consumers are not universally valid-qualified. Keep the\n  decoder's default-illegal policy and all checks on valid encodings;\n  sanitizing an invalid fetch must not legalize a valid illegal opcode.\n\n  Keep current hazard priorities. Raw source fields of an invalid\n  fetch may alias a load destination, but during imem backpressure the\n  PC is already held and either load-use flush or invalid-control\n  capture creates an inert ID/EX bubble. A real dmem hold preserves\n  the current instruction. An accepted EX redirect uses the existing\n  flush_id to squash the younger slot regardless of raw source fields.\n  Check these cases explicitly instead of depending on fabricated NOP\n  operands. Preserve reset, traps, x0, both bus handshakes, memory\n  effects, forwarded operands, nret=1 RVFI, contiguous order, and all\n  arithmetic and divider real/ALTOPS behavior and timing.\n\n  A minimal proposal-stage probe with direct instruction transport and\n  valid-qualified ID control capture achieved 49.478 MHz with Gowin\n  placement option 0, versus the reproduced champion's 46.361 MHz on\n  that option. Logic count was 2,875 versus 2,909, with 641 registers\n  in both. This preliminary timing result is not a complete formal or\n  CoreMark fitness evaluation. Expect unchanged bracketed cycle count\n  because accepted instructions and pipeline latencies are unchanged.\n  Acceptance requires median Gowin Fmax times measured iterations/cycle\n  greater than 108.68986; at 4,491,485 cycles for ten iterations this\n  means median Fmax greater than 48.81789 MHz. Estimate a 7 percent\n  fitness gain. The margin is modest; measure the full median and actual\n  marker-bracketed cycles rather than accepting one placement result.\n\n  Validate arbitrary raw instruction words while imemReady is zero,\n  followed by normal fetch resumption; taken branch/JAL/JALR recovery\n  over wrong-path loads, stores, illegal instructions, and divides;\n  coincident imem/dmem stalls; load-use aliases; divider holds; and\n  reset. Invalid fetches must never retire, launch division, write a\n  register, or cause a memory access. Compare valid retirement records\n  and cycle traces against the champion under identical ready streams.\n  Run existing unit, formal, stalled cosim, canonical CoreMark CRC/UART,\n  and marker checks without changing their contracts. Main risks are\n  incomplete invalid-control sanitization and remaining multiplier or\n  divider paths limiting the clock below the acceptance threshold.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry raw imem_data on out.instr independently of flush/redirect.\n      Preserve the current validity equation and every PC priority.\n      Document validity-based bubbles and ID/EX control sanitization.\n      Do not add squash-controlled instruction or source-address muxes.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      On allowed capture, store ctrl_decoded only for valid fetches;\n      otherwise store all-zero ctrl_t and clear valid. Preserve\n      reset/flush-over-hold priority, held operands and controls, valid\n      instruction decoding, and pipeline timing. Document why invalid\n      controls must be inert despite arbitrary instruction payloads.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add invalid-fetch payload cases resembling loads, stores,\n      branches, illegal instructions, and divides; source aliases\n      during imem stalls; recovery coincident with bus stalls; and\n      dmem/execute holds followed by release. Check exact accepted and\n      retired order, forwarded operands, traps, no wrong-path effects\n      or divide launch, no phantom load-use bubble, and unchanged\n      cycle timing under identical ready sequences. Preserve every\n      existing correctness assertion and divider progress bound.\n''')\nprint(p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
