{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat formal/checks_si.cfg\nsed -n '1,220p' fpga/core_bench_si.sv\nsed -n '1,200p' cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\"", "aggregated_output": "[options]\nisa rv32im\nnret 1\nsolver bitwuzla\n\n[depth]\n# BMC depth per check. 2nd column (where present) is the k-induction prove\n# depth. Keep depths matched to the current pipeline so bugs within ~20\n# cycles of any symbolic instruction stream are exposed.\ninsn          20\nreg       10  20\npc_fwd    10  20\npc_bwd    10  20\ncausal    10  20\n# unique   — no two retirements share rvfi_order (catches double-retire)\n# cover    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Same as checks.cfg. See checks.cfg for\n# the full rationale on what ALTOPS does and does not prove. M-extension\n# arithmetic correctness is covered by test/test_alu.py and `make formal-deep`.\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n# run_all.sh rebuilds this section from the actual rtl/ glob; the list\n# below is overwritten before genchecks runs. Kept as a placeholder so\n# the section is syntactically present for any reader inspecting the cfg.\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\n// fpga/core_bench_si.sv\n//\n// FPGA Fmax wrapper for single-issue (`nret 1`) cores. Same shape as\n// core_bench.sv but instantiates `core` with only the channel-0 RVFI port\n// set — no `_1` ports. Selected by the orchestrator (via BENCH env var in\n// synth.tcl) when cores/<target>/core.yaml declares nret: 1.\n//\n// See core_bench.sv for the rationale on LFSR-driven imem, the dmem\n// model, the V2 stall generator, and why the LED observes only the\n// memory-side outputs (RVFI-only logic is pruned).\nmodule core_bench (\n  input  logic clock,\n  input  logic reset,\n  output logic led\n);\n\n  logic [31:0]\n…[9241 of 12313 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ff1789dfa094bdf9]…\n   await _check(dut, ALU_SLT, 1, 0, 0)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SLT, 0x7FFFFFFF, 0x80000000, 0)     # MAX > MIN\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def sltu_unsigned(dut):\n    await _setup(dut)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SLTU, 0xFFFFFFFF, 0, 0)             # max > 0\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SLTU, 0, 1, 1)\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def sll(dut):\n    await _setup(dut)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SLL, 1, 4, 16)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SLL, 1, 31, 0x80000000)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SLL, 1, 0x20, 1)                    # shamt = b[4:0] = 0\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def srl(dut):\n    await _setup(dut)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SRL, 0x80000000, 1, 0x40000000)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SRL, 0x80000000, 31, 1)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SRL, 0xFFFFFFFF, 0x25, 0x07FFFFFF)   # shamt masked\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def sra(dut):\n    await _setup(dut)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SRA, 0x80000000, 1, 0xC0000000)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SRA, 0x80000000, 31, 0xFFFFFFFF)\n    await _check(dut, ALU_SRA, 0x7FFFFFFF, 1, 0x3FFFFFFF)\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
