{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_58", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check\ncat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r5s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\ntitle: \"Bank EX/MEM results and select them after the arithmetic registers\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 113.18 at median 47.574 MHz and 4,203,513 bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. The previous structural divider changes succeeded,\n  but the bottleneck has moved beyond the divider recurrence. A fresh\n  Gowin placement-option-0 run reproduces 46.485 MHz: the worst path is\n  26.807 ns from register-file BSRAM through operand selection, the\n  unsigned multiplier DSP, the execute result mux, and the data-memory\n  BSRAM address input. The next three paths start in divider remainder\n  state and end at the same memory address input, within 0.06 ns of the\n  worst path. Both arise because EX/MEM.alu_result carries arithmetic\n  results as well as effective addresses, and mem_stage drives it onto\n  dmem_addr even for non-memory instructions. Registering functional-unit\n  results before their common selection mux can shorten both arithmetic\n  paths. A dedicated registered memory-address channel is necessary to\n  preserve block-RAM inference when selection moves after the registers.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the single shared EX/MEM result register with parallel result\n  banks, moving functional-unit result selection after their registers.\n  This is one restructuring of the EX/MEM boundary by result producer and\n  consumer: an ordinary ALU bank, a divider-completion bank, a PC+4 link\n  bank, and a separately registered effective-address channel for memory.\n  Keep the same pipeline depth, instruction acceptance/retirement edges,\n  branch policy, forwarding interface, and nine-clock divide schedule.\n\n  In ex_stage, add three 32-bit result registers and a three-bit one-hot\n  registered selector. On reset clear all banks and the selector. On an\n  actual dmem stall hold every bank and selector together with EX/MEM.\n  Otherwise use the existing EX/MEM event priority: div_consume captures\n  div_result into the divider bank and selects it; execute_wait or invalid\n  EX input clears only t\n…[6692 of 9764 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:887505796c512d84]…\nps, prediction, forwarding, and arithmetic\n      schedules, including div_metadata_q and exactly-once completion.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use effective_addr for dmem_addr, alignment checks, byte enables,\n      load extraction, and aligned RVFI memory-address reporting. Retain\n      alu_result solely for its existing writeback role. Preserve request\n      enables, store data, load sign extension, and MEM/WB data retention.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add producer-bank transitions and immediate dependent consumers for\n      integer, multiply, divide, and link results; bubbles, held results,\n      reset, x0, and wrong-path cases. Distinguish effective addresses from\n      arithmetic results with forwarded bases, all memory widths/offsets,\n      negative immediates, misalignment, MMIO, load-use and bus stalls, and\n      held stores. Preserve existing arithmetic/progress assertions and\n      check exact RVFI operands, results, PCs, memory fields, and order.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
