{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_34", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r6s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-002-r6s1',\n 'title':'Retiming forwarding decisions into the ID/EX register',\n 'category':'structural',\n 'motivation':'''The champion scores 121.67 at median 51.146 MHz and 4,203,513\nmarker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its recorded worst path runs from\nregister-file storage through execute and branch recovery back to\nregister-file control. Prior structural divider improvements moved the\nbottleneck, and round-five result banking also exposed this recovery\npath. The current EX forwarding decision performs destination compares\nand priority selection before operand muxing, arithmetic, and branch\nresolution. Those register identities are already available one cycle\nearlier. Registering the selections with the consumer can remove this\ncontrol depth without increasing instruction or recovery latency.\n''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Move forwarding dependency resolution from EX into the preceding\ndecode interval, storing two two-bit selectors in ID/EX. Keep the actual\nforwarded data muxes in EX and retain their existing encoding: 0 selects\nthe captured register-file value, 1 selects EX/MEM.alu_result, and 2\nselects the MEM/WB writeback value. This is one retiming of the operand\nforwarding boundary; preserve the pipeline depth, predictor, recovery,\nload-use interlock, register file, address channel, and divider schedule.\n\nFor an instruction being accepted into ID/EX, compare its raw fetched\nrs1/rs2 fields against the current ID/EX destination for the next-cycle\nEX/MEM source, and against the current EX/MEM destination for the\nnext-cycle MEM/WB source. Use the existing forward_unit priority logic\nand x0 exclusions. Rewire its source inputs to io_imemData[19:15] and\nio_imemData[24:20], its first producer to id_ex_w.rd, and its second\nproducer to ex_mem_w.rd. Do not add a redirect-dependent instruction\nmask to these raw comparison inputs. EX receives the selectors stored\nin id_ex_w, not the live outputs of forward_unit.\n\nProducer eligibility must describe the control that will be registered\nat the receiving boundary, including trap suppression. Expose an EX\nnext_reg_write output equal to in.valid AND ctrl_with_trap.reg_write,\nand a MEM next_reg_write output equal to ctrl_with_trap.reg_write. Feed\nthese respectively to the existing comparator's ex_mem_w_en and\nmem_wb_w_en inputs. The EX flag includes jump-target misalignment\nsuppression; the MEM flag includes misaligned load suppression. Do not\nuse raw decoder reg_write for these decisions. MEM eligibility must\nretain the parent's distinction between forwarding state and retirement\nvalid; do not replace it with wb_w_en or add a mem_wb.valid qualifier.\n\nCapture both selectors on exactly the existing ID/EX capture edge.\nReset and flush clear them with the bundle; ID/EX holds retain them.\nWhen decode accepts, the older stages advance together, so the compared\nproducer identities become the selected sources on the next cycle.\nDuring a dmem hold, EX/MEM and the retained MEM/WB forwarding fields stay\nstable with ID/EX. During divider execution, source values are already\nsaved at request acceptance; keep that metadata capture and request/\ncompletion schedule unchanged. When the divider completes, a following\nconsumer captures selector 1 and receives the newly registered result.\nPreserve the load-use bubble: an immediately dependent load consumer is\naccepted only when the load will be in MEM/WB, selecting 2. Correctly\npredicted transfers, recovered transfers, imem bubbles and held slots\nmust keep their existing acceptance and retirement cycles.\n\nA proposal-stage implementation of this exact five-RTL-file change\nachieved Gowin Fmax values 51.936, 56.239, and 56.239 MHz for placement\noptions 0, 1, and 2. The median is 56.239 MHz, 9.96 percent above the\nchampion's 51.146 MHz. Option 0 uses 3,118 logic cells and 701 registers,\nwith eight LUT-RAMs, six BSRAMs and six DSPs. The existing pipeline\ncocotb runner passed all 27 cases. Full formal and stalled CoreMark\nvalidation are being checked separately; these timing results are\nprototype evidence, not tournament acceptance. The temporary RTL will\nbe restored before delivering this hypothesis for normal implementation.\n\nExpect approximately 10 percent higher fitness at unchanged bracketed\ncycles. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 127.26682, about\n53.499 MHz at the champion's cycle count. Principal risks are selector\nmisalignment under holds or bubbles, incorrect eligibility on a fault,\nand placement sensitivity with remaining divider and recovery paths.\nPreserve all top-level nret=1 ports, contiguous channel-0 retirement,\nx0, traps, illegal-by-default decoding, both memory handshakes, exact\nreal/ALTOPS arithmetic, and the existing ten-cycle progress bound.\nRun unchanged formal, real arithmetic tests, stalled retirement cosim,\nand canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/MMIO-bracket checks. Require unchanged\nmarker-bracketed cycles under the same bus-ready streams.\n''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':10,'confidence':'medium'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Add two two-bit forwarding-selector fields to id_ex_t only. Preserve\nthe existing bundle fields, external IO, and pipeline depth.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Accept the two next-cycle forwarding selections as input ports and\ncapture them with the instruction in ID/EX. Use the existing reset,\nflush, hold, and capture priorities without adding an independent\nselector update or altering operand capture.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Expose next_reg_write = in.valid && ctrl_with_trap.reg_write for the\ncurrent EX instruction, including jump alignment faults. Continue\nusing the existing forwarding muxes, now driven by ID/EX-registered\nselectors. Preserve arithmetic, branch recovery/training, independent\neffective_addr_q, and all divider request/metadata/completion logic.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Expose next_reg_write = ctrl_with_trap.reg_write, including memory\nalignment-fault suppression. Preserve all MEM/WB registers, held\nforwarding fields, memory requests and RVFI memory reporting.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Rewire the existing forward_unit to compare raw fetched source\naddresses against the current ID/EX and EX/MEM destinations, using\nthe new EX/MEM next-write eligibility outputs. Feed its selectors\ninto id_stage; feed id_ex_w's registered selectors into ex_stage.\nKeep hazard, data-bypass, retirement, and top-level interfaces intact.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain every existing architectural and progress assertion. Extend\nforwarding coverage for two producers targeting the same register,\nx0, simultaneous dependencies, load-use bubbles, divider completion,\nwrong-path and invalid instructions, and misaligned jump/load traps.\nExercise long dmem holds and imem gaps while comparing exact RVFI\nsource values, results, PCs, memory fields and retirement order. Add\nselector capture/hold checks using the stage-relative producer map.\n'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=78))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Wrote schema-valid hypothesis:',p)\nPY\ncat cores/bench/generated/r6s1/earlyfwd-formal.log; tail -6 cores/bench/generated/r6s1/earlyfwd-cosim.log\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
