{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_29", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml, json, jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r7s1.yaml')\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-002-r7s1',\n'title':'Use a narrow divider startup round to isolate sign correction',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 133.79 at median 56.239 MHz and 4,203,513 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its recorded worst placement-option-0 path runs from divider quotient_q[30] to EX/MEM alu_result[30], with 24.549 ns arrival and 80 cells. The existing divider chains two full-width radix-4 digits, result selection, and sign correction into its final transfer edge. Prior structural rounds r1s1, r2s1, and r3s1 succeeded by shortening divider arithmetic depth. Adding a correction clock would violate the existing ten-cycle ready-bus progress limit after an unpredicted transfer. Instead, exploit the small initial dividend prefix to finish the recurrence one clock earlier without extending execution latency.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Reschedule the blocking divider into a narrow eight-bit startup round, six normal four-bit rounds, and a separate correction/transfer round. Keep acceptance at edge 0 and EX/MEM transfer at edge 8, exactly the current nine EX clocks. This is one structural change to divider register placement and round scheduling; leave ordinary ALU/multiply execution, the registered forwarding selectors, branch policy, and the independent effective-address register unchanged.\n\nAt edge 0 capture the same unsigned operand magnitudes, full-width divisor and 3D, quotient dividend, signs, operation, and retirement metadata as the champion. At edge 1 divide the most-significant eight dividend bits by the captured divisor. If the divisor exceeds 255, this prefix is smaller than the divisor: emit an eight-bit zero quotient and retain the prefix as the remainder. Otherwise use four serial NARROW radix-4 digits over that eight-bit prefix. Each digit forms a ten-bit trial, subtracts D/2D/3D in parallel with eleven-bit differences, selects the largest non-borrowing candidate using one-hot logic, and retains its eight-bit remainder. D and 2D use the low eight divisor bits, widened before shifting; 3D uses the captured full multiple's low ten bits, sufficient when D <= 255. The full-width divisor test selects the bypass for all larger unsigned magnitudes. No combinational / or % operator is introduced.\n\nFor D=0, every narrow digit selects 3 and leaves the consumed prefix as its remainder, generating startup quotient 0xff. For nonzero D <= 255, the selected remainder stays below D and fits eight bits. At edge 1 write quotient_q = {old quotient_q[23:0], startup_quotient[7:0]} and zero-extend the startup remainder into remainder_q. At edges 2 through 7, execute the unchanged two full-width radix-4 digits per edge. Thus 8 + 6*4 = 32 dividend bits have been consumed and the COMPLETE unsigned quotient and remainder are registered immediately after edge 7. Keep the eight-state round counter schedule, but use the narrow path only when round_q==0 and the full recurrence when round_q==1 through 6.\n\nIn the final-round state round_q==7, select quotient_q or remainder_q directly and apply the existing sign correction; do not use quotient_next or remainder_next, which would perform an extra erroneous round. Assert div_valid from the same registered state/count as before. At edge 8 transfer this corrected result directly to EX/MEM, or capture it in the existing held-completion register if blocked. Preserve all request, busy, valid, consume, reset cancellation, and exactly-once metadata behavior. Preserve truncation toward zero, dividend-signed remainder, zero-divisor handling, and INT_MIN/-1. ALTOPS must retain its exact formulas and the identical nine-clock control schedule.\n\nThe long recurring path now ends at quotient_q/remainder_q, while sign correction starts from those registers and ends at EX/MEM or the held-result register. The four-digit startup path is narrow, and its early intermediate prefixes are at most two, four, and six bits. Measure its synthesized timing rather than assuming that narrow arithmetic guarantees a faster implementation. Principal risks are startup selection/routing, the added first-round mux on recurrence registers, magnitude-to-3D capture, or the unchanged multiplier and recovery paths becoming dominant.\n\nKeep nret=1 and all top-level ports, contiguous channel-0 retirement, x0, decoder defaults and legal encodings, traps, both memory handshakes, all existing checks, and the maximum ten-cycle ready-bus retirement gap. Update only divider-internal schedule assertions to the new consumed-prefix sizes: after intermediate edge e in 1..7, bits_consumed = 8 + 4*(e-1). Preserve every arithmetic expected-value assertion, availability check, nine-clock latency assertion, and progress bound. Cover D around 255/256, zero, high-bit unsigned divisors, signed magnitudes, all narrow digit thresholds, reset, held completion, competing requests, forwarding, consecutive divides, and a recovering jump followed by division. Run the existing real and ALTOPS unit suites, unchanged formal and stalled cosim, and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/MMIO-bracket gates.\n\nExpect unchanged CoreMark cycles with a higher global clock. Acceptance requires median Gowin Fmax times measured iterations/cycle strictly above 139.94434, approximately 58.828 MHz at the champion's cycle count. The expected gain is an estimate pending timing and full validation; do not weaken the progress contract or accelerate only ALTOPS to make the schedule pass.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':12,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/alu.sv','description':'''Add the narrow eight-bit startup division path with full-width large-divisor bypass. Use it for the first registered round, retain two full-width radix-4 digits for the next six rounds, and derive final corrected output from the completed registered quotient/remainder. Preserve edge-0 request acceptance, edge-8 transfer, sticky completion, reset, arithmetic corner cases, and identical ALTOPS scheduling. Update comments to document the nonuniform round widths.\\\\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_alu.py','description':'''Preserve all expected arithmetic, handshake, and total-latency assertions. Adapt intermediate prefix checks to 8,12,16,20,24,28,32 consumed bits. Add focused startup-boundary vectors around divisors 255/256, all four digit choices, high unsigned magnitudes, zero divisors, signed corner cases, changed operands while busy, blocked completion, and reset.\\\\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain existing architectural and ten-cycle progress assertions. Exercise forwarded startup operands, consecutive divisions, recovering transfers into divide instructions, reset, load/store backpressure, dependent consumers, and once-only source/result/metadata retirement under both bus stall streams.\\\\n'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=82))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Wrote and validated revised structural hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
