{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_60", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r8s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-002-r8s1',\n'title':'Decouple fetch from execution holds with an elastic instruction queue',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 133.79 at median 56.239 MHz and 4,203,513 bracketed CoreMark cycles. The pipeline currently freezes its fetch PC on a load-use interlock, data-memory stall or outstanding divide, discarding opportunities to fetch while the instruction bus is ready. Later independent instruction-bus stalls therefore become additional bubbles. Earlier structural register-file replacement regressed, while registered forwarding improved timing; the lessons also identify recovery-dependent instruction masking as a repeated timing problem. A small elastic fetch/decode boundary can improve useful work per cycle without adding a mandatory pipeline stage or changing arithmetic latency.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace the fused fetch/decode transport with a two-entry fall-through instruction queue. Separate instruction-bus fetch acceptance from decode consumption. Each entry contains the full PC, instruction and the direction prediction made for that fetch. When empty, bypass the live fetched instruction directly to decode, retaining the champion's no-stall latency and one-bubble branch-recovery penalty. When execution cannot advance, continue accepting ready instruction fetches until both entries are full. Buffered instructions may issue when io_imemReady is low. This is one structural change to the fetch/decode boundary; keep the existing predictor algorithm, ALU, divider, register file, memory stage and retirement width.\n\nMaintain a fetch PC distinct from the PC attached to the queue head. The fetch PC advances using the existing sixteen-entry agree predictor and direct-JAL prediction only when an instruction-bus response is accepted. Training remains tied to an advancing EX conditional branch. Latch predicted_taken in the queue entry at fetch acceptance and preserve it through arbitrary holds; never substitute a later table lookup. EX still compares the accepted instruction's saved prediction with its actual outcome.\n\nLet available = queue_nonempty || io_imemReady. The decode consumer uses the oldest buffered entry, or the live fetch when empty. Its acceptance is available && \"'!stall_if && !redirect && !reset, where stall_if now describes consumer load-use, execute-wait and data-memory conditions plus !available. Fetch acceptance is io_imemReady && (occupancy < 2 || consumer_accept) && !redirect && !reset. For an empty queue, simultaneous fetch and consumer acceptance bypasses storage and leaves occupancy zero. With one entry, simultaneous acceptance replaces the consumed head with the fetched entry; fetch-only appends and consume-only empties. With two entries, consuming moves the second entry to the head; a simultaneous fetch fills the newly freed tail. Full queues without consumption hold the fetch PC and all payloads. Do not allow duplicate enqueues when a held fetch address is repeatedly ready.\n\nReset clears occupancy and restores the existing reset PC. An accepted EX redirect has priority over all queue activity: discard every queued wrong-path entry, suppress decode acceptance and new enqueue on that edge, and load redirect_target even if io_imemReady is low. The target may bypass the empty queue on the next ready cycle. A correctly predicted transfer does not flush queued successors. A load-use bubble must retain the head; a data-memory hold or divider wait must retain it while allowing fetches into spare capacity. Older memory work and divider result handling remain unchanged.\n\nKeep the selected head'\"'s instruction data independent of flush and redirect. Emit its raw instruction and separate validity from IF; on a non-held invalid decode acceptance, clear the entire ID/EX bundle so no memory, forwarding or execution control leaks from an invalid word. Retain reset/flush priority and preserve every field on a true hold. This avoids reintroducing the known recovery-to-instruction-mask-to-register-file path at the new queue output. The same raw head, not the younger live imem word, supplies register-file addresses, immediate/decode inputs, load-use source comparisons and the registered-forwarding comparisons. Expose a separate available signal independent of masked validity so there is no combinational ready/flush loop. Rewire hazard_unit's existing imem_ready input to available; only the fetch unit consumes the external io_imemReady directly.\n\nPreserve the core's nret=1 port set, exact RV32IM and ALTOPS arithmetic, all legality/trap behavior, x0, contiguous retirement order and both external bus handshakes. Preserve nine EX clocks for division and the unchanged ten-cycle ready-bus progress bound. Queuing must never issue a stale instruction after recovery, duplicate a store, or retire an unaccepted response. No cache, extra outstanding bus protocol, branch predictor redesign, timing exception or evaluation change is proposed.\n\nA temporary two-entry prototype already reduced canonical stalled CoreMark to 3,987,564 bracketed cycles, a 5.14 percent reduction, and passed selftest retirement cosimulation with ready and stalled buses. Its first version retained the instruction-to-NOP recovery mux and measured only 51.190 MHz median, so that version did not improve fitness. The proposed validity-based head interface is being checked separately; cycle improvement alone is not acceptance. Final implementation must measure the complete vendor median and all correctness gates. Principal risks are queue-selection/ready fanout, changed prediction timing, and residual divider/multiplier timing limiting Fmax.\n\nRun unchanged formal including liveness and all canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker checks. Extend IF tests with an independent ordered queue scoreboard: empty bypass, fill, full hold, simultaneous consume/refill, prolonged independent bus stalls, changing invalid bus data, reset, correctly predicted transfers and redirect at every occupancy. Adapt pipeline monitors to distinguish fetched from issued PCs and use the accepted head for prediction/forwarding checks. Retain all architectural assertions and the ten-cycle progress bound; replace old transport-specific fixed cycle expectations with explicit queue and handshake expectations, never weaker correctness criteria. Check dependencies through load-use replay, division, WB drain, memory holds and recovery, with once-only retirement and stores. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 139.94434; at 3,987,564 cycles that requires Fmax above approximately 55.805 MHz.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Implement the two-entry fall-through queue, separate fetch and consumer acceptance, captured PC/instruction/prediction metadata, and redirect/reset invalidation. Preserve prediction and training policy. Emit a raw head plus separate availability/validity, and advance fetch PC only on accepted fetches.'},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Connect io_imemReady directly to the fetch unit and queue availability to the existing hazard-unit imem_ready input. Feed load-use and forwarding source comparisons from the raw selected queue head. Preserve producer eligibility, EX forwarding and every external port.'},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'Accept raw head instruction data without a recovery-dependent NOP mux. Clear the full ID/EX bundle on a non-held invalid acceptance; preserve flush/reset priority and exact hold behavior for operands, controls, forwarding selectors and prediction metadata.'},\n{'file':'test/test_if_stage.py','description':'Add an independent fetch-versus-consume queue scoreboard and directed occupancy, empty-bypass, full-hold, simultaneous refill, bus-ready, prediction metadata, redirect and reset checks. Keep all architectural PC/target and predictor correctness assertions.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Track fetched and issued instructions separately and check the raw accepted head for hazards and registered forwarding. Cover queued dependencies through load-use, divide and prolonged memory stalls, recovery at each occupancy, no wrong-path effects, exact RVFI and once-only retirement/stores. Preserve all progress and architectural checks.'}\n]}\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef rep(dumper,data): return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',data,style='|' if '\\\\n' in data else None)\nDumper.add_representer(str,rep)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Wrote and schema-validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
