IF; on a non-held invalid decode acceptance, clear the entire ID/EX bundle so no memory, forwarding or execution control leaks from an invalid word. Retain reset/flush priority and preserve every field on a true hold. This avoids reintroducing the known recovery-to-instruction-mask-to-register-file path at the new queue output. The same raw head, not the younger live imem word, supplies register-file addresses, immediate/decode inputs, load-use source comparisons and the registered-forwarding comparisons. Expose a separate available signal independent of masked validity so there is no combinational ready/flush loop. Rewire hazard_unit's existing imem_ready input to available; only the fetch unit consumes the external io_imemReady directly.\n\nPreserve the core's nret=1 port set, exact RV32IM and ALTOPS arithmetic, all legality/trap behavior, x0, contiguous retirement order and both external bus handshakes. Preserve nine EX clocks for division and the unchanged ten-cycle ready-bus progress bound. Queuing must never issue a stale instruction after recovery, duplicate a store, or retire an unaccepted response. No cache, extra outstanding bus protocol, branch predictor redesign, timing exception or evaluation change is proposed.\n\nA temporary two-entry prototype already reduced canonical stalled CoreMark to 3,987,564 bracketed cycles, a 5.14 percent reduction, and passed selftest retirement cosimulation with ready and stalled buses. Its first version retained the instruction-to-NOP recovery mux and measured only 51.190 MHz median, so that version did not improve fitness. The proposed validity-based head interface is being checked separately; cycle improvement alone is not acceptance. Final implementation must measure the complete vendor median and all correctness gates. Principal risks are queue-selection/ready fanout, changed prediction timing, and residual divider/multiplier timing limiting Fmax.\n\nRun unchanged formal including liveness and all canonical CoreMark C