{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_81", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r8s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-002-r8s1',\n'title':'Bank execute results after the registered-forwarding boundary',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 133.79 at median 56.239 MHz and 4,203,513 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst reported path runs from divider quotient state through 80 cells into the shared EX/MEM arithmetic-result register. Other recent paths enter that register through the multiplier. The r5s1 structural result-banking attempt improved its older parent by 5.85 percent but lost to another tournament candidate; its bottleneck moved to recovery/register-file control. Since then the champion has gained an independent registered memory address and retimed forwarding selectors, which changes the context for placing result selection after arithmetic registers. The proposal tests that single boundary change on the current champion, without increasing instruction latency.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace EX/MEM's shared arithmetic-result register with three parallel result banks: ordinary ALU/multiply, completed divide/remainder, and PC+4 link. Register a three-bit one-hot result selector with the same instruction. Drive out.alu_result from a parallel masked-OR of the registered banks. This moves functional-unit selection after the result flops and removes the divider/link selection mux from the ends of both the multiply and divider arithmetic paths. Keep the current ALU internals, registered forwarding decisions, register file, predictor, pipeline depth and nine-clock division schedule.\n\nOn reset clear all three banks and the selector. A real dmem stall holds all banks and the selector together with EX/MEM metadata. Otherwise use exactly the current EX/MEM event priority: div_consume captures div_result into the divide bank and selects it; execute_wait or an invalid EX input clears the selector and emits the existing inert metadata bubble; a normal jump captures in.pc+4 into the link bank and selects it; any other valid normal instruction captures the existing alu_result into the ordinary bank and selects it. Unselected banks retain their values. Do not introduce instruction-class-specific zero results or new operation gates on the ordinary bank. Preserve misaligned-jump handling and all metadata/trap controls exactly as today; suppressed writeback remains suppressed.\n\nEncode ordinary, divide and link selections as 001, 010 and 100. Compute the visible result as (alu_bank & replicate(sel[0])) OR (div_bank & replicate(sel[1])) OR (link_bank & replicate(sel[2])). The zero selector produces a zero bubble result. Remove the old functional writes to reg_q.alu_result so the synthesized circuit cannot retain a duplicate pre-register selection cone. Result selection must depend only on the registered selector, never the younger ID/EX instruction or live div_consume. Hold all selected data and metadata coherently under backpressure. Existing EX/MEM-to-EX forwarding and MEM/WB capture use the selected bank result on exactly their original cycles.\n\nKeep the champion's independent effective_addr_q register and its full-width forwarded rs1+imm input completely outside this bank selector. Its reset and dmem-hold-only behavior, and every mem_stage address/mask/extraction/alignment consumer, stay unchanged. Earlier result-banking work showed that routing dmem addresses through a post-register arithmetic mux can prevent Gowin BSRAM inference. The separate address channel is already present in this parent; do not reconstruct it or change its capture semantics.\n\nThis is a renewed test of a precise result-register topology after the parent forwarding boundary changed, not a claim that the old r5s1 measurements apply to the current core. Additional register bits and the post-register forwarding mux can move timing pressure to operand selection, branch recovery or register-file control. Preserve the current forwarding selectors and trap-aware producer eligibility. Do not combine this with divider-radix changes, a multicycle multiplier, a fetch queue or register-file remapping.\n\nPreserve nret=1 and all external ports, exact RV32IM and RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS arithmetic, default-illegal decoding, trap behavior, x0, both bus handshakes, contiguous channel-0 retirement and every existing progress bound. Divide acceptance, captured operands/metadata, final-round availability and completion retention must be unchanged. A waiting or invalid instruction must still leave all memory controls inert. No formal-depth, liveness, memory-wrapper, benchmark or timing-constraint changes are permitted.\n\nA temporary implementation on this champion passed the unchanged pipeline cocotb suite, selftest retirement cosimulation with ready and stalled buses, and canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/MMIO-bracket validation. It measured exactly 4,203,513 bracketed cycles for ten iterations, matching the champion. Final vendor timing and unchanged formal must determine whether the new register placement provides an accepted improvement. These are proposal-stage checks, not tournament acceptance. Temporary RTL is restored after investigation; implement this topology from the champion and rerun the gates.\n\nExtend tests by alternating ordinary integer, multiply, divide, JAL and JALR producers followed immediately by dependent ALU operations, branches, stores and memory-base consumers. Cover x0, faults, bubbles, long dmem holds, simultaneous imem stalls, divide completion after backpressure, reset and recovery. Check exact forwarded operands, source metadata, bank selection, results, RVFI order/PCs, and once-only retirement and stores. Keep every existing assertion and the ten-cycle progress bound. Expect unchanged CoreMark cycles. Acceptance requires median Gowin Fmax times measured iterations/cycle strictly above 139.94434, approximately 58.826 MHz at the champion's cycle count. Report measured timing honestly even if the result misses that threshold.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Replace the shared EX/MEM ALU/divider/link result destination with separately enabled ALU, divide and PC+4 register banks and a registered one-hot selector. Use existing reset, hold, divide-completion, bubble and normal-advance priorities. Select the visible result after the registers, leaving metadata, forwarding timing, traps and the independent effective-address register unchanged.'},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Retain all existing timing, architectural, forwarding, memory and progress assertions. Add directed transitions between all result banks with immediate dependent consumers, held selection under dmem stalls, division completion, reset, bubbles and faults; require identical results, source metadata, order and retirement cycles.'}\n]}\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef rep(dumper,data): return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',data,style='|' if '\\\\n' in data else None)\nDumper.add_representer(str,rep)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Final candidate written and schema-valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
