{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(d['id'],d['title'], 'fitness', d.get('fitness'), 'cycles', d.get('cycles'))\n  print(h.get('motivation','') if isinstance(h,dict) else '')\n  if d['id'].endswith(('r4s2','r7s2','r8s2','r9s2')):\n   print(h.get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 Instruction-aware bimodal prediction for the fused fetch/decode stage fitness 12.97 cycles 4202272\nThe baseline always fetches PC+4 and redirects on every taken conditional\nbranch and every jump. Despite the five-stage description, IF/ID is a\ncombinational bundle, so each such redirect discards one younger fetch/decode\nslot. CoreMark's repeated loops and calls offer an opportunity to eliminate\nthose bubbles. The only recorded experiment is the 12.12 baseline; there is\nno LESSONS.md or prior predictor result. Its measured worst path runs through\nforwarding and the combinational ALU, so this proposal targets iterations\nper cycle while keeping predictor logic out of that arithmetic path.\n\nhyp-20261003-003-r2s2 Apply instruction-aware bimodal prediction to the multicycle-divider champion fitness 74.95 cycles 4202272\nThe current champion already removes the dominant combinational divider\npath and scores 71.56 at 32.14 MHz, but still fetches sequentially and\nsquashes a younger fetch/decode slot on every taken branch or jump.\nIF/ID is combinational, so each unnecessary redirect loses one slot,\nnot the two suggested by the textbook five-stage description.\nLESSONS.md and log.jsonl show that hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 reduced CoreMark\ncycles from 4,491,485 to 4,202,272 with a 128-entry bimodal predictor and\ndirect-JAL prediction. That candidate retained the old combinational\ndivider and reached only 5.452 MHz; its regression against the champion\ndoes not demonstrate a predictor failure. Applying this measured cycle\nimprovement to the current multicycle-divider parent is the strongest\nevidence-backed predictor opportunity, with roughly 6.9% fitness upside\nif the current clock frequency is maintained.\n\nhyp-20261003-003-r3s2 Timing-lean bimodal prediction with direction-only recovery fitness 96.91 cycles 4202272\nThe current champion scores 93.33 at 41.919 MHz and 4,491,485 bracketed\nCoreMark cycles. Its fused combinational IF/ID stage loses one younger\ninstruction slot whenever EX redirects a taken branch or \n…[25593 of 28665 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:35c08cd47c30378d]…\nnical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation and all scored Gowin\nplacement options before judging success. These are proposal-stage\nmeasurements, not tournament acceptance. The deliverable is this YAML;\ntemporary RTL and test probes are restored after validation.\n\nhyp-20261003-003-r10s2 Hold a registered predictor correction PC until fetch acceptance fitness 126.04 cycles 4203513\nThe champion's sixteen-entry agree predictor already leaves only about\n55,124 conditional misses and 13,735 JALRs in 4,203,513 CoreMark cycles,\naccording to prior proposal tracing. Accuracy alone therefore offers\ntoo little headroom for the 4.6 percent acceptance margin. Experiments\nr6s2 through r9s2 reduced pieces of recovery or lookup logic but retained\ncompeting EX-to-fetch/decode timing paths. In particular, r6s2 registered\nthe younger-slot kill while still repairing the ordinary PC directly\nfrom combinational EX. Cut both parts of that recovery feedback at a\nregister boundary while preserving the single lost execute slot.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
