{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261003-003-r3s2','hyp-20261003-003-r10s2']:\n  print(d['id']); print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261003-003-r3s2\nAdd an instruction-aware 128-entry, two-bit bimodal direction predictor\nwith unconditional direct-JAL prediction, but implement it with local\ncounter updates and just one saved prediction bit per instruction.\nThis is one predictor architecture change. Preserve the champion's\narithmetic, nine-clock divider schedule, pipeline boundaries, memory\nbehavior, decoder legality rules, and nret=1 retirement contract.\n\nIndex the counters by fetch PC[8:2], initialize them to weakly not\ntaken (01), and use the selected MSB to predict legal conditional\nbranches. Derive full-width signed B/J targets from the current raw\nimem_data and fetch PC; predict legal direct JAL taken unconditionally.\nOnly select a direct target when it is word aligned. All other\ninstructions, including JALR and reserved branch encodings, predict\nPC+4. No cached targets, global history, return stack, or extra stage\nare needed. Generate predictions independently of redirect-masked\ninstruction outputs to avoid feedback through flush and hazards.\n\nImplement the table as 128 small constant-index counter cells in a\ngenerate loop. Each cell has its own synchronous enable comparing\nthe EX update index with its constant index, and computes saturation\nfrom its own two state bits. Only the MSBs feed the asynchronous\nfetch-indexed lookup. Avoid a variable-index read-modify-write path\nthat multiplexes a second 128-entry table read into shared update\nlogic and fans that result back to all cells. Read-before-write is\nsufficient; do not add a same-cycle update bypass. Train exactly once\nwhen a valid, legal, non-faulting