{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,86p' Makefile && rg -n 'single-bit|squash|recovery' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl --max-count 1\"", "aggregated_output": ".PHONY: help lint test test-infra cosim formal fpga timing next loop report bench clean\n.DEFAULT_GOAL := help\n\n# Resolve OSS CAD Suite path so commands work even when the user hasn't\n# sourced .toolchain/oss-cad-suite/environment in this shell.\nTOOLCHAIN_DIR := $(CURDIR)/.toolchain\nOSS_BIN       := $(TOOLCHAIN_DIR)/oss-cad-suite/bin\nLOCAL_BIN     := $(TOOLCHAIN_DIR)/bin\nexport PATH := $(OSS_BIN):$(LOCAL_BIN):$(PATH)\n\n# Orchestrator knobs (pass-through to tools.orchestrator):\n#   N        — number of rounds for `make loop` (default 10).\n#   K        — tournament size, slots per round (default 1 = sequential).\n#   AGENT    — codex (default) or claude. Honors a pre-existing\n#              AGENT_PROVIDER env var if AGENT isn't set on the make\n#              command line.\n#   BASE     — when forking a new TARGET, copy from cores/<BASE>/.\n#              Default: baseline (orchestrator-side default).\n#   COREMARK — target CoreMark iterations/sec (e.g. '370').\n#   LUT      — target LUT count (e.g. '3000').\n#   WORKTREE — default ON. `make loop` first creates a dedicated git worktree\n#              at .worktrees/<TARGET>/ on branch core-<TARGET> and re-execs\n#              itself there. Lets two `make loop` invocations for different\n#              TARGETs run in parallel without git index/merge collisions.\n#              To opt out (run directly on the current branch + cwd) pass\n#              an explicit empty value: `make loop TARGET=v1 WORKTREE=`.\nN        ?= 10\nK        ?= 1\nAGENT    ?= $(or $(AGENT_PROVIDER),codex)\nBASE     ?=\nCOREMARK ?=\nLUT      ?=\nWORKTREE ?= 1\n\n# Compose optional CLI flags for the orchestrator. Empty vars produce\n# empty strings so the orchestrator falls back to its defaults.\nORCH_FLAGS  = --iterations $(N) --tournament-size $(K)\nifneq ($(strip $(BASE)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --base $(BASE)\nendif\nifneq ($(strip $(COREMARK)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --coremark-target $(COREMARK)\nendif\nifneq ($(strip $(LUT)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --lut-target $(LUT)\nendif\n\n# Multi-core: TARGET selects the core under cores/<TARGET>\n…[13269 of 16341 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:86be75917a92727d]…\n, \"fitness\": 12.97, \"delta_pct\": 7.01, \"vs_baseline\": 7.01, \"fmax_mhz\": 5.452, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4202272, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 12934, \"ff\": 749, \"seeds\": [5.27, 5.452, 5.452], \"perturbations\": [[0, 5.27, 145], [1, 5.452, 145], [2, 5.452, 145]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.ctrl.reg_write_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -184.744, \"arrival_ns\": 195.038, \"logic_cells\": 148, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 145, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-03T16:09:29.338726Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 (regression, +7.01%): A 128-entry bimodal predictor plus direct-JAL prediction cut CoreMark cycles by 6.4% by removing redirect bubbles, but the unchanged forwarding/combinational-ALU critical path kept Fmax near 5.45 MHz, limiting the fitness gain over baseline to 7%.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"The existing tests enforce a ten-cycle progress bound for a redirected divide, so an extra recovery bubble would violate that check. I’ve refined the design: keep immediate PC redirection, but delay the squash to a registered EX kill bit. This should cut the same control path while preserving the current miss penalty."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r6s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Retimed predictor squash with immediate PC repair and registered EX annulment'\nd['motivation']='''The champion's recorded worst path crosses EX branch resolution, IF recovery masking and register-file control. Its sixteen-entry agree predictor already captures most available direction benefit: prior architectural tracing estimated only 55,124 conditional misses and 13,735 JALRs against 4,203,513 bracketed cycles. Accuracy alone offers too little headroom. The previous direct-target reuse attempt gained only 1.60 percent. Retiming the predictor's squash action, while keeping PC repair immediate, can break the feedback path through fetch decoding and register-file reads without adding a misprediction bubble or violating the existing ten-clock divide-successor progress bound.'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Keep the existing sixteen-entry agree predictor and all target and direction calculations. Split recovery into immediate PC repair and a one-cycle-delayed annulment of the wrong-path EX slot. A valid, advancing EX misprediction still redirects the IF PC register to resolved_pc_next at the same edge as today. However, that combinational redirect must no longer mask the IF instruction, alter source-register addresses, clear IF valid/predicted_taken, or flush ID/EX. Instead, register a single kill_ex bit from the accepted redirect. No new target register, predicted-target metadata, target mux or ordinary pipeline stage is needed.\n\nOn the misprediction edge, the resolving branch/jump advances normally to EX/MEM, the PC captures its architectural successor, and the current younger wrong-path instruction may enter ID/EX. During the following cycle, kill_ex annuls that younger EX instruction before any side effect while fetch/decode already sees the repaired PC. At the next edge, EX/MEM receives a complete inert bubble and ID/EX accepts the repaired target instruction. This is the same single lost execute slot as the present design, which inserts that bubble directly into ID/EX one edge earlier. Correct predictions remain unchanged.\n\nImplement kill_ex as reset-to-zero state assigned from redirect every clock; redirect itself is qualified by \"'!kill_ex, valid, !reset, !stall and !execute_wait. Suppress predictor training and further recovery for the annulled EX slot. Exclude kill_ex instructions from is_div so they cannot launch or consume division, assert execute_wait, or capture divider metadata. In the EX/MEM update, preserve reset and older dmem-hold priority, then clear the entire result/control/valid bundle when kill_ex is asserted. Do not merely clear valid because memory and forwarding consumers are not uniformly validity qualified. Keep the independently controlled effective-address register on its original reset/hold/capture schedule; its unused sum is harmless with memory controls zero.\n\nThe annulled younger instruction must not trigger a load-use stall for the repaired target. Qualify the existing id_ex_mem_read input to the hazard unit with !kill_ex, or apply equivalent gating inside the hazard unit. Remove immediate redirect from flush_if and flush_id: flush_if remains imem backpressure, and flush_id remains the existing qualified load-use bubble. Preserve data-memory and divider holds and MEM/WB forwarding-data retention. The resolving transfer is not a load, so immediate PC recovery cannot conflict with a real load-use hazard. The one-cycle kill cannot be lost to an older dmem hold: it is generated only on accepted EX transfer advance, and that transfer occupying EX/MEM in the kill cycle has no memory request. Still retain the original older-memory stall priority.\n\nIn IF, retain reset then immediate redirect then ordinary accepted prediction as PC-register priorities. Restrict IF payload masking and prediction-valid suppression to reset/existing imem flush behavior; immediate redirect must not feed the instruction-to-NOP mux, out.valid or out.predicted_taken. The wrong-path instruction'\"'s direction metadata may be captured but is ignored by kill_ex. On an imem-stalled recovery edge, the PC still repairs and an invalid slot may be admitted; kill_ex remains harmless. On the following cycle, an imem stall simply postpones target acceptance without changing repair or retirement. Keep raw-instruction lookup and all normal acceptance-edge metadata holds.\n\nPreserve EX training exactly once on the resolving non-faulting branch's advance. Preserve resolved architectural pc_next and original PC+4 links, JALR masking, target alignment traps, decoder legality, all arithmetic and ALTOPS schedules, full addresses, nret=1, and every retirement/memory invariant. Do not relax the existing ten-clock ready-bus retirement gap or formal liveness depth; redirected division must retain its current latency.\n\nExpect about 6 percent higher fitness through Fmax with unchanged bracketed cycles. At 4,203,513 cycles, beating fitness 121.67 by more than 4.6 percent needs roughly 53.50 MHz rather than 51.146 MHz. This is an unmeasured estimate. The main risks are moving the critical path to multiply/divide or the new EX kill gating, and routing/placement variation. Measure the full Gowin median-Fmax times actual bracketed iterations/cycle product.\n\nValidate unchanged formal and stalled cosim gates, canonical CoreMark CRC/UART, bounds and MMIO brackets. Update prediction scoreboards to represent the one transient speculative ID/EX slot and delayed kill, retaining all architectural assertions and progress bounds. Check exactly one bubble per miss, unchanged correct-prediction throughput and redirected-divide latency, no wrong-path memory/divider activity, no wrong-path predictor training or second redirect, and exact contiguous RVFI retirement. Include wrong-path loads whose rd aliases the repaired target's sources, stores, divides, branches, illegal instructions, adjacent transfers, misaligned targets, dmem release, imem-stalled repair, and reset with kill pending.'''\nd['changes']=[\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Add a reset-cleared one-cycle kill_ex register driven by the accepted immediate redirect, expose it to hazard qualification, and annul the younger EX slot before EX/MEM capture. Gate is_div, EX advance, recovery and predictor training against kill_ex. Preserve immediate resolved PC repair, full-bundle inert bubbles, older-memory holds, all arithmetic and the independent effective-address schedule.'},\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'Keep immediate redirect exclusively on PC-register update. Remove redirect from combinational instruction masking, valid and predicted_taken suppression; imem flush behavior remains. Preserve the agree table, raw instruction lookup and all target calculations and PC priorities.'},\n {'file':'rtl/hazard_unit.sv','description':'Remove immediate redirect-driven IF/ID flushes; retain imem flush and ordinary qualified load-use bubble. Ignore annulled ID/EX load controls using the registered kill bit so they cannot stall the repaired target. Preserve all memory/divider holds and writeback forwarding retention.'},\n {'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'Wire the registered EX annulment bit to load-use qualification, while immediate redirect and resolved target continue to feed only IF PC repair. Adapt internal hazard wiring as needed, preserving external memory ports and the sole channel-0 RVFI port set.'},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Model the transient wrong-path ID/EX slot and registered EX kill in the predictor scoreboard. Assert one-shot branch updates/recovery, unchanged single-bubble miss cost and existing ten-clock liveness bound, no wrong-path memory/divider/training/retirement, and correct target acceptance despite killed load-use dependencies. Exercise recovery across both bus stalls, division, adjacency, faults and reset without weakening existing architectural assertions.'},\n {'file':'test/test_if_stage.py','description':'Adapt recovery-edge payload expectations to permit an unmasked speculative instruction while retaining immediate redirect-over-stall PC priority. Preserve counter saturation, read-before-write, acceptance-edge lookup and reset tests.'}\n]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Updated and schema-validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
