{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_55", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema,textwrap\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r6s2.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Move predictor squash to a registered EX kill bit and keep fetch data unmasked'\nd['hypothesis']=d['hypothesis'].replace('Qualify the existing id_ex_mem_read input to the hazard unit with \"'!kill_ex, or apply equivalent gating inside the hazard unit.'\"', 'Qualify the existing id_ex_mem_read input to the hazard unit with id_ex_w.valid && \"'!kill_ex, or apply equivalent gating inside the hazard unit. Invalid imem slots now carry raw decoded controls, so both validity and annulment qualification are required.'\"')\nd['hypothesis']=d['hypothesis'].replace('Restrict IF payload masking and prediction-valid suppression to reset/existing imem flush behavior; immediate redirect must not feed the instruction-to-NOP mux, out.valid or out.predicted_taken.', 'Remove the instruction-to-NOP mux entirely: out.instr = imem_data, out.valid = \"'!flush, and out.predicted_taken = predicted_taken && !(reset || flush), where flush is now imem backpressure only. Thus recovery and unavailable fetches are represented by registered validity/kill metadata, while the instruction data and register-file read addresses remain unmasked. Immediate redirect must not feed these combinational payload fields. Retain the existing EX execute_wait || !in.valid full-bundle bubble rule so arbitrary controls decoded from an invalid fetch cannot reach EX/MEM; only a valid but annulled slot uses control-and-valid-only clearing.'\"')\nold='''Expect about 6 percent higher fitness through Fmax with unchanged bracketed cycles. At 4,203,513 cycles, beating fitness 121.67 by more than 4.6 percent needs roughly 53.50 MHz rather than 51.146 MHz. This is an unmeasured estimate. The main risks are moving the critical path to multiply/divide or the new EX kill gating, and routing/placement variation. Measure the full Gowin median-Fmax times actual bracketed iterations/cycle product.'''\nnew='''A temporary prototype of this exact raw-fetch, separate-kill-bit design measured Gowin placement frequencies of 49.260, 52.089 and 52.089 MHz, for a median of 52.089 MHz. Option-0 logic usage was 3,106 with 708 registers, eight LUT-RAMs, six BSRAMs and six DSPs. The adapted pipeline and IF cocotb suites passed, including an added killed-load dependency and wrong-path side-effect test that preserves the ten-clock redirected-divide bound. The unchanged cosim evaluator passed both available ELFs, including stalled selftest comparison and canonical CoreMark validation. The unchanged CoreMark IPC evaluator measured exactly 4,203,513 bracketed cycles for ten iterations, equal to the champion.\n\nThese measurements project fitness 123.9178, approximately 1.85 percent above 121.67. Expect about 2 percent improvement, with low confidence in acceptance: this measured prototype does NOT clear the required 4.6 percent margin. Acceptance at unchanged cycles requires Fmax strictly above about 53.497 MHz. The remaining measured critical path crosses the register file, ID and combinational ALU into EX/MEM, so eliminating predictor-recovery feedback alone may not provide enough clock headroom. Report this limitation honestly and rerun all unchanged gates during implementation; do not weaken checks or claim the candidate has won.\n\nImplementation details matter: retaining the imem-to-NOP mask measured only 48.764 MHz; killing the entire EX/MEM data bundle measured 48.157 MHz. For the proposed design, leave the original invalid-input/divide bubble schedule intact, use control-and-valid-only clearing specifically for a valid annulled instruction, and remove both redirect and imem masking from the instruction data bus. Keep the separate kill bit and original ID/EX validity capture; folding repair into that validity bit was a different, slower probe. Temporary RTL and test edits are restored after measurements; implement the YAML proposal normally.'''\nassert old in d['hypothesis'];d['hypothesis']=d['hypothesis'].replace(old,new)\nd['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':2,'confidence':'low'}\nd['changes'][1]['description']='''Keep immediate redirect exclusively on PC-register update. Drive out.instr directly from imem_data, removing the wide NOP mux for both recovery and unavailable fetches. Keep out.valid = \"'!flush and out.predicted_taken = predicted_taken && !(reset || flush), with flush representing imem backpressure. Preserve agree counters, raw instruction lookup, full target arithmetic and PC priorities.'\"'''\nd['changes'][2]['description']='''Remove immediate redirect-driven IF/ID flushes and the unused redirect port; retain imem flush and the qualified load-use bubble. Load-use detection must ignore both invalid and annulled ID/EX instructions. Preserve all older-memory/divider holds and writeback forwarding retention.'''\nd['changes'][3]['description']='''Wire the registered EX kill bit to hazard qualification: id_ex_mem_read = id_ex_w.valid && id_ex_w.ctrl.mem_read && \"'!kill_ex. Immediate redirect and resolved target continue to feed IF PC repair, and no longer feed the hazard unit. Preserve external memory and sole channel-0 RVFI ports.'\"'''\nd['changes'][5]['description']='''Adapt recovery-edge and unavailable-fetch payload expectations to raw instruction data and valid/prediction metadata. Retain immediate redirect-over-stall PC priority and all counter saturation, read-before-write, acceptance-edge lookup and reset tests.'''\nclass D(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef string_representer(dumper,value):\n return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',value,style='|' if '\\\\n' in value else None)\nD.add_representer(str,string_representer)\nfor k in ('motivation','hypothesis'):\n d[k]='\\\\n\\\\n'.join(textwrap.fill(q,width=74) for q in d[k].strip().split('\\\\n\\\\n'))+'\\\\n'\nfor c in d['changes']:\n c['description']=textwrap.fill(c['description'],width=72)+'\\\\n'\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=D,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nassert d['id']=='hyp-20261003-003-r6s2' and d['category']=='predictor'\nprint('Schema valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
