{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_32", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r7s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-003-r7s2',\n 'title':'Prepare misprediction recovery addresses from the saved prediction',\n 'category':'predictor',\n 'motivation':'''The champion's sixteen-entry agree predictor already removes most control-flow bubbles. Prior tracing estimated only 55,124 conditional misses and 13,735 JALRs in 4,203,513 bracketed cycles, so additional accuracy alone has little acceptance headroom. Direct-target reuse and delayed EX annulment failed the 4.6 percent margin. Current EX recovery unnecessarily uses resolved_pc_next as redirect_target: a late forwarded-operand branch comparison selects a full-width address before recovery reaches the PC and fetch controls. The saved prediction already identifies the only possible destination on a direction mismatch.''',\n 'hypothesis':'''Separate the speculative recovery-address datapath from the architectural resolved-next-PC datapath. Retain the existing sixteen-entry agree predictor, direct-JAL prediction, direction-XOR misprediction detection, immediate recovery, and single-bubble miss penalty. This is one predictor-recovery change: prepare the address of the opposite prediction before the branch condition resolves.\n\nIn ex_stage define the recovery address as in.predicted_taken ? (in.pc + 32'd4) : (in.ctrl.is_jump ? jump_target : branch_target). Keep redirect = in.valid && ex_advance && (actual_taken \"'^ in.predicted_taken) unchanged. When redirect is asserted, actual_taken is exactly the inverse of the saved prediction, so this new address is exactly resolved_pc_next on every edge on which IF may use it. A predicted-taken branch that is actually not taken repairs to PC+4; a predicted-not-taken branch that is actually taken repairs to its direct target; an unpredicted legal JALR repairs to the forwarded rs1+imm target with bit zero cleared. Direct JAL behavior is unchanged. Do not consult the current counter MSB for recovery: use the exact prediction captured with this instruction.\n\nThe two addresses are intentionally allowed to differ while redirect is deasserted. Keep resolved_pc_next'\"'s current actual-outcome calculation and use it exclusively for the architectural EX/MEM pc_next and RVFI pc_wdata. Preserve original PC+4 link writes, forwarded operand selection, full-width branch/JAL arithmetic, JALR masking, legality, misalignment suppression, and the current sequential architectural successor on traps. Do not replace architectural pc_next with the recovery address. A hypothetical predicted-taken instruction that resolves to a trap still repairs to PC+4; normally direct-target alignment checks suppress such predictions before acceptance.\n\nKeep ex_advance qualified by \"'!reset && !stall_ex_mem && !execute_wait, and preserve reset then accepted redirect then ordinary accepted prediction as IF PC priority. Recovery must still win over imem backpressure on dmem release and squash the younger slot before any side effect. Prediction metadata continues to hold with its instruction through all memory, load-use and divider stalls. No new stage, target register, comparison, BTB, return stack, delayed update packet or additional miss cycle is introduced. Counter training, reset and saturation remain identical. Preserve nret=1, divider schedules and arithmetic, all memory requests and retirement invariants.\n\nThis removes the actual branch outcome from the wide recovery-address selection, reducing comparison fanout and one late mux dependency in the EX-to-IF feedback path. The actual comparison still determines the one-bit redirect enable, as required. The current recorded worst path is arithmetic, so frequency improvement is uncertain and must be established by vendor timing rather than assumed from logic depth. Initially estimate five percent higher fitness with low confidence; accuracy and CoreMark cycle count should be unchanged. At 4,203,513 cycles, acceptance over 133.79 requires a median Fmax above approximately 58.827 MHz. Judge the measured median-Fmax times bracketed iterations/cycle product and retain every formal, canonical CRC/UART, address-bound and timing-marker gate.'\"''',\n 'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':5,'confidence':'low'},\n 'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Drive redirect_target from the saved predicted_taken bit: sequential PC+4 for an originally taken prediction, otherwise the existing jump/branch target. Leave direction mismatch qualification, architectural resolved_pc_next, RVFI, training, traps, links, arithmetic and advance/hold behavior unchanged. Document that recovery and architectural next PC must match whenever redirect is asserted, but need not match otherwise.'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Add a recovery-address assertion against independently resolved architectural next PC whenever redirect is accepted, while retaining existing retirement and miss-cost checks. Cover false-taken and missed-taken branches, all branch comparisons, direct JAL, JALR with forwarded and load-produced bases, target equal to PC+4, negative displacements and carry/wrap boundaries, misaligned transfers and illegal encodings, adjacent transfers, dmem holds/release, imem-stalled recovery, divider holds, and reset. Check that RVFI pc_wdata remains actual-outcome based on correctly predicted as well as mispredicted instructions and that wrong-path stores/divides never execute.'''}]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Replaced hypothesis with prediction-derived recovery address proposal; schema valid.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
