{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_56", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r8s2.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261003-003-r8s2',\n'title':'Look ahead one fetch to register the agreement-predictor lookup',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The champion already removes most control-flow bubbles with sixteen agreement counters and direct-JAL prediction. Earlier target-reuse, delayed-squash and opposite-offset recovery experiments failed the acceptance margin. Enlarging the predictor offers little cycle headroom: prior tracing estimated 55,124 conditional misses and 13,735 JALRs in 4,203,513 bracketed cycles. A distinct timing opportunity is the asynchronous counter lookup inside the current fetch-PC feedback loop. Move that lookup across the existing PC register boundary by preparing a single agreement bit for the destination PC.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Keep the sixteen two-bit agreement counters, immediate EX training, displacement-sign bias, direct-JAL prediction, and direction-XOR recovery. Add exactly one predictor state bit, predict_agree_q, alongside the fetch PC. It stores the counter MSB read for the destination selected at the previous PC-update edge. Conditional prediction becomes the current raw instruction's displacement sign XNOR predict_agree_q, still qualified by legal BRANCH encoding and aligned direct target. Direct JAL remains unconditionally predicted when aligned; JALR remains sequential.\n\nRead the counter MSBs for the three possible next fetch destinations in parallel: sequential_index = pc[5:2] + 4'd1, direct_target[5:2], and redirect_target[5:2]. Register the selected one-bit result with exactly the PC register's priorities: reset sets predict_agree_q to one, otherwise redirect selects the recovery row even during stalls, otherwise \"'!stall selects the direct-target row when predicted_taken or the sequential row when not taken; otherwise hold. Use parallel indexed reads followed by one-bit selection, rather than selecting a full address or a four-bit index before one common lookup. The table remains sixteen local counter cells with an MSB-only read vector and no new memory primitive.\n\nThe PC and agreement snapshot must always advance or hold together. Preserve the full 32-bit PC and existing target additions; the four-bit sequential-index increment is only predictor indexing and wraps modulo sixteen. A redirect to the same PC still refreshes the snapshot. Reset sets both PC zero and snapshot one, matching the counters'\"' weak-agree 2'b10 initialization. Do not insert an instruction stage, delay recovery, change branch resolution, or register the instruction memory interface.\n\nUse read-before-write semantics with no EX-training bypass: at a PC-update edge, the snapshot reads the old counter state even if that same row trains at that edge. While PC is held for imem backpressure, load-use, dmem freeze or division, keep its snapshot unchanged even if older branches update its table row. This deliberately freezes a speculative direction hint for the resident PC. At eventual instruction acceptance both next-PC selection and captured predicted_taken must use that exact held bit. EX remains authoritative and recovers every actual direction mismatch, so stale snapshots can affect accuracy but cannot alter architectural behavior. Never recompute captured prediction metadata from the live table during a hold.\n\nKeep EX training on each valid, legal, non-faulting conditional branch advance exactly as in the champion, updating the cell's current two-bit state. Preserve reset, saturation, no training from held or wrong-path instructions, immediate recovery over imem stalls, complete younger-slot squash, accepted-instruction metadata holds, architectural pc_next and link writes, traps, and the existing ten-clock ready-bus progress bound. Preserve nret=1, external ports, registered forwarding, independent effective-address capture, all real/ALTOPS arithmetic, decoder checks, memory handshakes and retirement order.\n\nThe intended timing mechanism removes the live 16:1 table selection from the current-PC-to-predicted-next-PC path, at the cost of one register and three narrow lookups. Prediction refresh and recovery-index routing may instead become critical, and the unchanged multiplier/divider can still cap Fmax. This is a timing hypothesis rather than a claim of greater predictor accuracy.\n\nAn initial common-index lookahead prototype passed selftest cosimulation and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation at 4,203,504 cycles for ten iterations, but measured only 53.311 MHz median Gowin Fmax. That projects about 126.83 fitness, roughly 5.2 percent below the 133.79 champion. The proposed parallel-read implementation is being checked separately; do not claim acceptance from this initial result. The expected impact is conservatively negative pending that measurement. Acceptance requires a measured product strictly above 139.94434, about 58.826 MHz at this cycle count. All unchanged correctness and vendor-timing gates remain mandatory.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':-5,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/if_stage.sv','description':'''Add a reset-to-one predict_agree_q register whose hold and update priorities exactly match PC. Read sequential, direct-target and recovery counter MSBs in parallel, then select the registered one-bit snapshot. Use this bit in conditional prediction instead of a live table lookup. Keep all sixteen counter cells, EX training, branch legality/alignment, target arithmetic, output masking, PC repair and prediction-metadata equations otherwise unchanged. No training bypass or snapshot refresh during a PC hold.'''\n },\n {'file':'test/test_if_stage.py','description':'''Extend the independent reference model with the captured agreement bit and read-before-write semantics. Check reset and matching PC/snapshot updates for sequential, predicted-direct and recovery destinations, index wraparound, redirect-to-same-PC, recovery overriding every hold, simultaneous same-row training, and old snapshots retained while held counters change. Preserve counter saturation, legality, alignment and PC-priority checks; explicitly assert that eventual acceptance uses the snapshot, not the live row.'''\n },\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Adapt only the predictor timing model to the registered snapshot and retain all architectural, retirement, memory and progress assertions. Cover adjacent aliased branches, warmed loops, loop exits, full-width targets, JAL/JALR, wrong-path stores/divides/illegal instructions, load-dependent branches, long dmem holds, imem-stalled recovery and division. Check exact instruction/prediction pairing and once-only training. Run unchanged formal, stalled cosim and canonical bracketed CoreMark validation.'''\n }\n]}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated and schema-validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
