tion, alter source-register addresses, clear IF\nvalid/predicted_taken, or flush ID/EX. Instead, register a single kill_ex\nbit from the accepted redirect. No new target register, predicted-target\nmetadata, target mux or ordinary pipeline stage is needed.\n\nOn the misprediction edge, the resolving branch/jump advances normally to\nEX/MEM, the PC captures its architectural successor, and the current\nyounger wrong-path instruction may enter ID/EX. During the following\ncycle, kill_ex annuls that younger EX instruction before any side effect\nwhile fetch/decode already sees the repaired PC. At the next edge, EX/MEM\nreceives a complete ine\n…[10096 of 13168 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e4ac77470a055ba4]…\necorded 56.239 MHz median, confirming the comparison.\nThese are proposal-stage measurements, not tournament acceptance. The\ntemporary RTL and test probes have been restored; implement the proposal\nnormally from the champion.\n\nThat measured product is fitness 127.9858, approximately 4.34 percent BELOW\nthe 133.79 champion. The integer expected impact is consequently -4 percent,\nwith low confidence in an eventual improvement. This hypothesis does NOT clear\nthe required 4.6 percent acceptance margin. At unchanged cycles it would need\nmedian Fmax strictly above approximately 58.826 MHz. Retain this explicit\nnegative result rather than claiming that shorter logical feedback implies a\nfaster FPGA. Implementation must rerun all unchanged gates and the scored\nmedian; do not relax checks or accept below the required fitness product.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION NOTES: \nhyp-20261003-003-r8s2 Look ahead one fetch to register the agreement-predictor lookup {'fitness': 123.83, 'delta_pct': -7.44, 'fmax_mhz': 52.053, 'cycles': 4203504}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
