{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('category')=='micro_opt':\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print(d['id'], d['title'], d.get('fitness'),d.get('outcome'))\n  if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261003-001-r9s0','hyp-20261003-001-r12s0','hyp-20261003-001-r11s0','hyp-20261003-001-r4s0','hyp-20261004-001-r14s0']:\n   print(h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-683f32f Baseline retest for cores/bench 12.12 improvement\nhyp-20261003-001-r1s0 Share and width-specialize the combinational divider 9.28 regression\nhyp-20261003-001-r2s0 Reduce the iterative divider to six restoring steps per clock 90.35 regression\nhyp-20261003-001-r3s0 Shorten restoring-divider borrow chains with a balanced block prefix 67.06 regression\nhyp-20261003-001-r4s0 Use validity at ID/EX instead of muxing fetched instructions to NOP 112.47 regression\nCarry imem_data directly in the combinational IF/ID instruction field\non every cycle. Keep out.valid = !(flush || redirect), the existing\nPC update, and redirect-over-stall priority. Move invalid-instruction\nneutralization to the receiving ID/EX register: reset or explicit\nflush clears the whole payload; otherwise a stall holds it; otherwise\ninvalid IF/ID clears the whole payload; otherwise decode and capture\nnormally. This replaces the wide instruction-to-NOP mux and its\nredirect-dependent source addresses with narrow validity control at\nthe register boundary. It is one local bubble-insertion optimization,\nwith no changed pipeline stages, branch policy, or instruction latency.\n\nIn id_stage preserve the exact precedence with, for example:\nif (reset || flush) reg_q <= '0;\nelse if (!stall && !in.valid) reg_q <= '0;\nelse if (!stall) capture the existing decoded fields;\notherwise hold. Do not clear a held instruction merely because the\ncurrent fetch is invalid. A dmem-stalled or divider-held instruction\nmust survive simultaneous imem backpressure. Clear every control in\nan accepted bubble, not just valid: memory and forwarding units use\nsome controls without independently checking valid. Do not retain\ndecoded load/store/reg_write controls from an invalid raw instruction.\n\nThe hazard unit will now see raw fetched rs fields. Preserve its\nequations and priorities. If fetch is stalled, PC already holds and\nID either captures an inert bubble or the load-use condition inserts\none. If dmem or execute waits, existing stall priority holds ID/EX.\nOn a rea\n…[25301 of 28373 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f2c1ac6fb5e90493]…\n-time modulo expressions against zero and naming conditional generate blocks. Those lint-only source corrections were made after the reported P&R run; rerun scored timing during implementation. Full formal and CoreMark checks are being attempted within the proposal budget; any completed results will be appended below. No assertion was weakened.\n\nAn earlier radix-16 prototype retained M's last radix-4 digit and measured only 81.879 MHz, below the acceptance margin. Implement the COMPLETE-result construction above, not that earlier prefix variant. The expected gain comes from removing the serial division work at both boundaries while keeping the same operation schedule. Added divisor-multiple registers and parallel subtractor routing are material risks, as are newly close branch, multiplier and ordinary-ALU forwarding paths. Acceptance requires measured fitness strictly above 200.21486, about 84.872 MHz at unchanged cycles. Temporary RTL and test edits are restored after research; this YAML is the deliverable.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
