{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_19", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261003-003-r6s2','hyp-20261003-003-r9s2','hyp-20261003-003-r12s2']:\n  print(d['id'])\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261003-003-r6s2\nKeep the existing sixteen-entry agree predictor and all target and\ndirection calculations. Split recovery into immediate PC repair and a one-\ncycle-delayed annulment of the wrong-path EX slot. A valid, advancing EX\nmisprediction still redirects the IF PC register to resolved_pc_next at\nthe same edge as today. However, that combinational redirect must no\nlonger mask the IF instruction, alter source-register addresses, clear IF\nvalid/predicted_taken, or flush ID/EX. Instead, register a single kill_ex\nbit from the accepted redirect. No new target register, predicted-target\nmetadata, target mux or ordinary pipeline stage is needed.\n\nOn the misprediction edge, the resolving branch/jump advances normally to\nEX/MEM, the PC captures its architectural successor, and the current\nyounger wrong-path instruction may enter ID/EX. During the following\ncycle, kill_ex annuls that younger EX instruction before any side effect\nwhile fetch/decode already sees the repaired PC. At the next edge, EX/MEM\nreceives a complete inert bubble and ID/EX accepts the repaired target\ninstruction. This is the same single lost execute slot as the present\ndesign, which inserts that bubble directly into ID/EX one edge earlier.\nCorrect predictions remain unchanged.\n\nImplement kill_ex as reset-to-zero state assigned from redirect every\nclock; redirect itself is qualified by !kill_ex, valid, !reset, !stall and\n!execute_wait. Suppress predictor training and further recovery for the\nannulled EX slot. Exclude kill_ex instructions from is_div so they cannot\nlaunch or consume division, assert execute_wait, or capture divider\nmetadata. In the EX/MEM update, preserve reset and older dmem-hold\npriority, then clear all control bits and validity for the annulled slot\nwhile letting unused datapath fields capture normally. Do not add a kill-\ncontrolled clear to arithmetic result data. Do not merely clear valid\nbecause memory and forwarding consumers are not uniformly validity\nqualified. Keep the independently controlled effective-address regis\n…[14801 of 17873 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bda1724c12569914]…\nt RVFI cosimulation passed with ready\nand stalled buses; canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation\npassed at exactly 4,203,513 bracketed cycles for ten iterations.\nThese are proposal-stage measurements, not tournament acceptance. The\nintended mechanism did not remove the remaining register-file to EX/MEM\narithmetic and divider-completion bottlenecks. The measured product is\nabout 126.74 fitness, approximately 5.3 percent BELOW\n133.79. Expected impact is therefore -5 percent with low confidence in\nimprovement; this candidate is not an observed speedup.\n\nRerun all unchanged formal and stalled cosim gates, canonical CoreMark\nCRC/UART/bounds/marker validation, and the scored Gowin median during\nimplementation. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 139.94434;\nat 4,203,513 bracketed cycles for ten iterations it requires median Fmax\nabove approximately 58.826 MHz. Do not relax correctness or progress\nchecks. Restore temporary prototype RTL after research; this YAML is\nthe sole proposal deliverable.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
