g steps in\\neach divider round are therefore a direct target for local timing\\noptimization. The previo\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261003-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Retiming division to four restoring steps with fused completion\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The structural champion hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 scores 71.56 at 32.14 MHz.\\nIts recorded critic\n…[14517 of 17589 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9a08ffb0554ef5f7]…\nence are close competing limits. Local control-cone probes moved the worst paths into the unchanged recurrence without im\n42:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261004-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Overlap bus stalls with a two-entry fall-through instruction ring\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The champion reaches 191.41 iterations/sec at 81.140 MHz and 4,239,051\\nbracketed cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. Fetch still stops whenever\\ndecode waits for a dependency, a divide or data memory, and decode cannot\\nuse previously available instructions during an instruction-bus stall.\\nSt\n43:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261004-003-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Distributed target prediction for direct branches and last-target JALR\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current champion still computes predicted direct targets through the\\ninstruction-memory word and a full-width B/J immediate adder. The r13s2\\nsixteen-entry direct-only BTB removed that recurrence, but added 33,452\\nCoreMark cycles from cold/conflict misses and left JALR unpredicted. Us\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
