{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_34", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-001-r15s0.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nd['title']='Decouple late-result dependency stalls from EX target-fault resolution'\nd['motivation']='''The champion scores 202.72 at 85.936 MHz and 4,239,051 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Fresh Gowin option-zero timing reproduces 88.137 MHz: the worst path goes from ID/EX rs2 through branch comparison, EX/ID control and the fetch PC clock enable. The shared x_write eligibility includes branch-target fault resolution, even though the only instructions that can request a late-result stall are loads and M operations. This creates a logically unnecessary branch-comparison dependency in the front-end interlock. Earlier recovery optimizations were limited by arithmetic paths that subsequent accepted changes have shortened; the current divider recurrence remains a close competing limit.'''\nd['hypothesis']='''Separate unfinished-writer dependency detection from the trap-aware forwarding-write eligibility. This is one local Boolean control-cone optimization, with no predictor change, arithmetic change, new stage, new state or changed instruction schedule.\n\nIn ex_stage redefine result_pending as a qualified unfinished writer: in.valid && \"'!in.ctrl.is_illegal && in.ctrl.reg_write && (in.ctrl.mem_read || is_mul || is_div). Keep is_mul/is_div and every request/consume/execute_wait equation unchanged. This signal depends only on captured instruction controls and validity, not on branch comparisons, target addresses or ctrl_with_trap. Loads defer alignment checking until MEM; legal MUL/DIV/REM operations cannot generate an EX control-transfer alignment fault. A branch or jump cannot be an unfinished writer under the validated decoder.\n\nIn forward_unit compute operand_wait independently of the forwarding selectors: x_pending && (x_rd != 0) && ((d_rs1 == x_rd) || (d_rs2 == x_rd)). Remove the two assignments that accumulate operand_wait inside x_write-qualified selector arms. Leave both forwarding priority trees unchanged: a trap-aware X writer wins over M, otherwise M wins over the register file with its W bypass. Preserve next_reg_write and its full fault suppression exactly. Do not replace x_write globally with a raw reg_write bit, because misaligned jumps must never forward or write a link value. Preserve comparisons against both raw instruction fields, including unused fields, to retain exact RVFI operand behavior and the existing cycle count.\n\nFor every reachable pipeline state a pending writer is a valid legal load or M instruction and therefore also passes the existing EX writer eligibility. Thus the independent interlock equals the original interlock, but removes branch-result and misalignment logic from the PC and ID/EX hold-control cones. Preserve the complete hazard-unit equations, youngest-writer blocking, control-bubble insertion, dmem/divider holds, recovery-over-imem priority, ID/EX operand stability, nret=1, reset, traps and all real/ALTOPS arithmetic. No RVFI-only optimization or contract change is included.\n\nValidate the implication result_pending -> next_reg_write using the actual decoder and reachable control combinations, then prove or exhaustively check old versus new operand_wait and unchanged forwarding selectors for all source/destination addresses and producer kinds. Cover x0, X-over-M aliases, all load/M variants, illegal encodings, non-writing branches, aligned and misaligned JAL/JALR, taken/not-taken relations and overlapping holds. Run the unchanged pipeline/IF suites, formal/liveness and stalled CoreMark gates, retaining canonical CRCs, UART text, memory bounds and marker timing. Expect exactly 4,239,051 bracketed cycles; measure them. Acceptance requires fitness strictly above 212.04512, approximately 89.889 MHz at unchanged cycles.\n\nThe intended gain is shorter global hold control and reduced fanout from EX fault resolution. Vendor median timing must establish the result: the radix-16 recurrence and ordinary ALU-to-ID bypass paths are already nearly tied with recovery and can cap improvement. This proposal is being probed; no measured gain is claimed yet. Temporary RTL experiments will be restored, leaving this YAML as the deliverable.'\"'''\nd['changes']=[{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'Make result_pending denote a valid, legal, register-writing load or M operation, using captured controls only. Preserve trap-aware next_reg_write, branch logic and every arithmetic/handshake equation.'},{'file':'rtl/forward_unit.sv','description':'Compute operand_wait directly from qualified x_pending, nonzero x_rd and either raw source-address match. Remove x_write dependence only from the wait equation; retain both existing trap-aware X-over-M forwarding selector trees.'},{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'Add targeted interlock equivalence checks across pending/nonpending producers, X/M/W aliases, x0, faulting jumps, raw unused source fields and overlapping bus/divider holds. Keep exact operand, trap, retirement, no-side-effect and progress assertions.'}]\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=100))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Updated and validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
