{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_97", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r15s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261004-002-r15s1\ntitle: \\\"Decouple fetch acceptance with an instruction ring and registered annulment\\\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion scores 202.72 at 85.936 MHz and 4,239,051 bracketed\n  CoreMark cycles. Structural attempt r14s1 demonstrated 5.77 percent\n  fewer cycles with a two-entry instruction ring, but its clock loss kept\n  fitness below acceptance. The current radix-16 divider removes that\n  attempt's reported worst arithmetic recurrence. A proposal-stage\n  ring-only probe on this champion still reached just 78.969 MHz:\n  buffering leaves EX branch resolution feeding forwarding and backend\n  hold into ring and PC enables. The proposed structural partition adds\n  the control boundaries needed to preserve elastic fetch's clock speed.\nhypothesis: |\n  Repartition fetch/decode acceptance into a two-entry fall-through\n  instruction ring whose backend hold is independent of resolved branch\n  outcomes. Register wrong-path annulment at X while retaining immediate\n  PC repair. Separate pending-producer interlocks from completed-result\n  forwarding. These are the data, recovery and backpressure parts of one\n  elastic front-end boundary. Keep the existing predictor, resolved ID/EX\n  operands, EX/MEM arithmetic partition and nine-clock divider unchanged.\n\n  In if_stage, use exactly two fixed 65-bit slots containing\n  {pc,instruction,predicted_taken}, a two-bit count, and separate one-bit\n  read_slot/write_slot pointers. Select the head with a bitwise masked OR\n  of slot zero for count\"'!=0 AND !read_slot, slot one for count!=0 AND\n  read_slot, and the live tuple for count==0. Neither recovery nor bus\n  readiness may mask instruction data or source-register addresses.\n  Preserve prediction as a fetch-time snapshot in each buffered tuple.\n  Keep io_imemAddr directly driven by the fetch PC register.\n\n  Define backend_hold as operand_wait OR execute_wait OR the actual\n  older dmem hold, excluding instruction-bus readiness. With stall equal\n  to backend_hold, pop = count!=0 AND !stall;\n  fetch_accept = imemReady AND (count!=2 OR pop);\n  push = fetch_accept AND (count!=0 OR stall).\n  A ready live instruction bypasses an empty ring without moving either\n  pointer. Buffered instructions can issue during imem stalls; fetch can\n  fill free slots during dependencies, division and dmem waits. Toggle\n  each pointer only for its buffered pop or push; update count only for\n  push-only or pop-only. Support full simultaneous pop/push with\n  read-before-write behavior. Write only slots[write_slot] on push; never\n  compact payloads or derive the write pointer from occupancy.\n\n  Reset or accepted recovery clears count and both pointers, with priority\n  over normal queue events. Payload writes remain controlled solely by\n  push: a simultaneous discarded write is harmless with count cleared.\n  Do not reset payload data or put redirect on their data/write enables.\n  Recovery repairs the fetch PC immediately even when imemReady is low;\n  otherwise advance PC only on fetch_accept. Fetch/decode valid is\n  count!=0 OR imemReady, independent of immediate redirect. Existing IF\n  flush can be tied low; raw head data remains visible when invalid.\n\n  Replace immediate IF/ID squashing with a separate kill_ex register in\n  ex_stage: reset it to zero; otherwise, whenever EX/MEM is not held by\n  actual dmem backpressure, set it to the accepted redirect. The recovery\n  edge may admit a speculative head into ID/EX, but the next X slot is\n  annulled while the repaired target is already at decode. Qualify is_mul,\n  is_div, next_reg_write and ex_advance with !kill_ex. A killed slot must\n  not launch/consume division, assert execute_wait, redirect, train the\n  predictor or forward. When it advances, clear the entire EX/MEM ctrl\n  bundle, m_kind and valid, while leaving unused data captures intact.\n  Preserve the dedicated effective-address register'\"'s original schedule.\n  Hold kill_ex through any actual EX/MEM hold. A normal recovery follows\n  an advancing branch/jump, so its next M slot cannot itself stall dmem.\n  Keep the existing single lost X slot per miss and target JALR link\n  forwarding from M. Do not add a recovery-target register or PC input mux.\n\n  Remove redirect from hazard_unit's flush_if/flush_id and from IF output\n  validity/prediction gating. Dependency flushing remains operand_wait\n  AND \"'!dmem_hold AND !execute_wait. Keep ID/EX resolved operand captures,\n  complete inert controls on invalid slots, and immutable operands during\n  holds. Connect both forward_unit source comparisons to the selected\n  head instruction rather than raw io_imemData. Only resolve operands\n  when that head issues; never buffer register values or bypass choices.\n\n  Complete the boundary by computing result_pending as valid AND\n  !kill_ex AND !decoded_illegal AND decoded_reg_write AND\n  (mem_read OR is_mul OR is_div), without resolved branch/jump trap input.\n  In forward_unit, compute operand_wait independently of the forwarding\n  priority arms as result_pending AND a match between the X destination\n  and either nonzero D source. Retain trap-aware next_reg_write and the\n  existing youngest-writer priority for data selection. Pending load/M\n  writers still block consumers rather than exposing an older value;\n  branches/jumps are never pending producers. This removes the spurious\n  branch-comparator-to-forwarding-to-ring-hold timing path without\n  relaxing fault suppression or dependency correctness.\n\n  Preserve nret=1, x0, both memory handshakes, exactly-once requests and\n  retirement, contiguous RVFI order, exact source metadata, target and\n  memory misalignment behavior, decoder legality and EBREAK discipline.\n  Leave all integer/M arithmetic, ALTOPS formulas, divider latency and\n  operand immutability unchanged. Train the sixteen-entry agree predictor\n  exactly once for each advancing legal non-faulting conditional branch.\n\n  The complete temporary RTL prototype passed strict Verilator lint and\n  unchanged selftest retirement cosimulation with ready and stalled buses.\n  Canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/bounds/marker validation passed.\n  It measured 3,994,432 bracketed cycles for ten iterations and Gowin\n  placement frequencies 81.604, 87.038 and 87.038 MHz, giving a scored\n  median of 87.038 MHz. The product is 217.89831 iterations/sec, a measured\n  7.49 percent gain over 202.72 and above the 212.04512 acceptance floor.\n  Option zero used 4,543 logic cells, 1,086 registers, 40 LUT RAMs, four\n  BSRAMs and two DSPs. Remaining worst paths include M multiply completion\n  and the ring read-pointer through register-file reads to resolved D\n  operands. The timing improvement is measured for this complete\n  partition; a ring alone or annulment alone did not clear acceptance.\n\n  Validate FIFO order, all push/pop combinations, full-queue recovery,\n  imem-stalled repair, immutable queued predictions, both source ports,\n  younger-writer priority, wrong-path loads/stores/divides/branches/illegal\n  instructions, reset cancellation, and target dependencies on killed\n  writers. Adapt internal fetch monitors to separate enqueue from issue\n  and to model the speculative X slot and registered annulment. Preserve\n  all architectural checks, arithmetic expectations, and the maximum\n  ten-clock ready-bus retirement-gap assertion, including redirect directly\n  to DIV/REM. Run all unchanged formal and cosim gates and the complete\n  scored Gowin median during implementation. Temporary RTL/test probes\n  are restored after research; this YAML is the deliverable.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the exact two-slot 65-bit ring, count, separate read/write\n      pointers, masked-OR live/head mux and distinct fetch acceptance.\n      Clear only queue metadata on recovery. Keep payload enables free\n      of redirect, preserve fetch-time predictions, and remove immediate\n      recovery from decode-valid and predicted_taken gating.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the held/resettable kill_ex bit, qualify every X producer and\n      side effect, and annul EX/MEM controls/kind/valid. Keep immediate PC\n      repair and arithmetic data paths. Generate pending eligibility only\n      from captured instruction class and validity, excluding resolved\n      branch/jump fault logic from the backend interlock.\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Compute operand_wait directly from qualified pending eligibility\n      and nonzero source/destination matches outside the forwarding\n      priority arms. Preserve trap-aware youngest-writer data selection.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Express backend hold independently of imemReady and redirect.\n      Remove immediate recovery flushes; retain load/M dependency bubbles,\n      divider holds and real dmem backpressure. Fetch validity comes from\n      ring occupancy or live readiness, and kill_ex annuls recovery slots.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire imemReady directly into the ring, pass backend hold separately,\n      compare forwarding sources from if_id_w.instr, and update hazard\n      ports. Preserve the nret=1 I/O and all backend arithmetic connections.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Extend the independent predictor scoreboard with FIFO acceptance,\n      immutable prediction snapshots, all push/pop transitions, full\n      recovery, reset and buffered consumption during imem stalls.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Model fetch and decode-head PCs separately plus the registered kill\n      slot. Retain retirement, operand, request, fault and progress checks;\n      cover killed-writer aliases, queue recovery and divide holds without\n      asserting that the independent fetch PC freezes with the backend.\nYAML\npython3 - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-002-r15s1.yaml')\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Schema valid:',h['id'],h['category'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
