{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['id'],d['title'],'fitness',d.get('fitness'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'),'fmax',d.get('fmax_mhz'))\n  if d['id'].endswith('r4s2'):\n   print(json.dumps(d['hypothesis'],indent=2))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 Instruction-aware bimodal prediction for the fused fetch/decode stage fitness 12.97 cycles 4202272 fmax 5.452\nhyp-20261003-003-r2s2 Apply instruction-aware bimodal prediction to the multicycle-divider champion fitness 74.95 cycles 4202272 fmax 31.494\nhyp-20261003-003-r3s2 Timing-lean bimodal prediction with direction-only recovery fitness 96.91 cycles 4202272 fmax 40.725\nhyp-20261003-003-r4s2 Sixteen-entry agree prediction with instruction-derived branch bias fitness 113.18 cycles 4203513 fmax 47.574\n{\n  \"motivation\": \"The current champion scores 103.91 at 46.673 MHz and 4,491,485 bracketed\\nCoreMark cycles. All three previous predictor attempts reduced cycles to\\n4,202,272, but their timing cost prevented selection over the concurrent\\narithmetic champions. Even the direction-only, locally updated 128-entry\\npredictor in r3s2 lowered Fmax from its own parent's 41.919 to 40.725 MHz.\\nIts 6.9% instruction-throughput benefit leaves only about 2.1% allowable\\nclock loss above the 4.6% acceptance threshold. The promising next step is\\nto retain that accuracy with a substantially smaller direction table.\\n\\nA proposal-stage reference-interpreter trace of the canonical compiled\\n2K CoreMark program contains 529,588 conditional branches, 49,367 JALs,\\nand 13,735 JALRs between the MMIO markers. Offline direction replay gave\\n54,452 misses for a cold 128-entry bimodal table and 55,124 misses for a\\ncold 16-entry agree table biased by the instruction's displacement sign.\\nThis supports reducing counter storage from 256 bits to 32 bits with\\nalmost unchanged accuracy. These are architectural trace-model results,\\nnot measured RTL cycles, formal results, or FPGA timing measurements.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Add a compact instruction-biased agree predictor to the existing fused\\nfetch/decode stage. This is one predictor architecture change: replace\\nabsolute taken/not-taken learning with learning agreement with a branch's\\nown static direction bias, allowing just sixteen two-bit counters indexed\\nby fetch PC\n…[7699 of 10771 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:09ffabf1fdea7561]…\nr's direct target through an acceptance-latched target field fitness 114.99 cycles 4203513 fmax 48.336\nhyp-20261003-003-r6s2 Move predictor squash to a registered EX kill bit and keep fetch data unmasked fitness 123.92 cycles 4203513 fmax 52.089\nhyp-20261003-003-r7s2 Preselect the opposite prediction offset for immediate branch recovery fitness 127.99 cycles 4203513 fmax 53.799\nhyp-20261003-003-r8s2 Look ahead one fetch to register the agreement-predictor lookup fitness 123.83 cycles 4203504 fmax 52.053\nhyp-20261003-003-r9s2 Compile the saved prediction into a direct branch-miss predicate fitness 134.61 cycles 4203513 fmax 56.584\nhyp-20261003-003-r10s2 Hold a registered predictor correction PC until fetch acceptance fitness 126.04 cycles 4203513 fmax 52.98\nhyp-20261003-003-r11s2 Register prediction recovery and defer the resolving transfer by one cycle fitness 125.7 cycles 4271994 fmax 53.699\nhyp-20261003-003-r12s2 Confine immediate prediction squash to ID/EX controls fitness 125.3 cycles 4203513 fmax 52.671\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
