{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,shutil\nprobe=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/34139506/07e02362/r14s2_btb64_rtl');probe.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nfor p in Path('cores/bench/rtl').glob('*.sv'):shutil.copy2(p,probe/p.name)\nfor p,s in json.loads(Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/34139506/07e02362/r14s2_btb32.json').read_text()).items():\n if p=='rtl/if_stage.sv':\n  s=s.replace('logic [31:0] btb_valid','logic [63:0] btb_valid').replace('logic [24:0] btb_tag','logic [23:0] btb_tag').replace('[0:31]','[0:63]').replace('logic [4:0] lookup_index','logic [5:0] lookup_index').replace('[6:2]','[7:2]').replace('[31:7]','[31:8]')\n  s=s.replace('  input logic [31:0] btb_update_pc, btb_update_target,', '  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\\\\n  input logic [31:0] btb_update_pc, btb_update_target,\\\\n  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */')\n  s=s.replace('  logic [23:0] btb_tag', '  (* syn_ramstyle = \\\"distributed_ram\\\" *) logic [23:0] btb_tag').replace('  logic [29:0] btb_target', '  (* syn_ramstyle = \\\"distributed_ram\\\" *) logic [29:0] btb_target').replace('  logic [1:0] btb_kind', '  (* syn_ramstyle = \\\"distributed_ram\\\" *) logic [1:0] btb_kind')\n (probe/Path(p).name).write_text(s)\nPY\npython3 - <<'PY' > /srv/hwebench/tmp/34139506/07e02362/r14s2_timing_btb64.log 2>&1\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nw=Path.cwd();t=Path('/srv/hwebench/tmp/34139506/07e02362')\nr=build_all(w,t/'r14s2_btb64_rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',t/'r14s2_btb64_gen')\nprint(json.dumps(r));print(json.dumps(summarize(r)))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_61", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r14s2.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261004-003-r14s2\ntitle: \\\"Distributed target prediction for direct branches and last-target JALR\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion still computes predicted direct targets through the\n  instruction-memory word and a full-width B/J immediate adder. The r13s2\n  sixteen-entry direct-only BTB removed that recurrence, but added 33,452\n  CoreMark cycles from cold/conflict misses and left JALR unpredicted. Use\n  a larger explicitly distributed target table on the present champion,\n  including last-target JALR prediction, to retain the timing benefit with\n  fewer recovery events. The existing sixteen-entry agree table is already\n  accurate enough that increasing direction capacity alone offers little\n  chance of clearing the fitness margin. RAM inference is material: an\n  unannotated 32-entry probe inferred extra BSRAM and fell to 66.714 MHz;\n  explicit distributed storage reached 82.122 MHz with identical behavior.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace instruction-derived next-PC prediction with one 32-entry,\n  direct-mapped, fully tagged branch target buffer covering conditional\n  branches, JAL and legal JALR. Index by PC[6:2]; each entry stores a\n  25-bit PC[31:7] tag, a 30-bit target[31:2], and two kind bits containing\n  {unconditional_jump, displacement_sign}. Maintain a separate 32-bit\n  valid vector. Use asynchronous lookup and one synchronous write port\n  for each payload array. Apply the Gowin-supported SystemVerilog\n  attribute (* syn_ramstyle = \\\"distributed_ram\\\" *) to all three payload\n  arrays; reset only validity, leaving payload unreset. This exact mapping\n  requirement avoids the extra block-RAM implementation measured in the\n  unannotated probe. Require fetch PC[1:0]==0 as part of a full-PC hit.\n\n  Retain the existing sixteen local agreement counters, PC[5:2] indexing,\n  reset state, saturation and training discipline. On a matching branch\n  entry, predict taken when the saved displacement sign equals the live\n  agree-counter MSB. On a matching JAL/JALR entry, predict its saved target\n  unconditionally. A miss predicts PC+4. The JALR entry learns the last\n  resolved target, so returns or other indirect transfers may miss when\n  their destination changes. Do not add a return stack or another history\n  predictor. Remove raw-instruction predecode and the immediate target\n  adder from the IF next-PC cone completely; do not retain a miss fallback.\n  Keep io_imemAddr driven directly by the registered PC.\n\n  Train or replace an entry exactly once on valid, legal, non-faulting EX\n  advance of a conditional branch, JAL or JALR, and only if the resolved\n  target is word aligned. Conditional branches allocate PC+immediate on\n  either outcome; do not allocate a misaligned conditional target even\n  when the branch was not taken. JALR stores the resolved rs1+immediate\n  target after bit-zero clearing. Write tag, target, kind and validity\n  atomically at the resolving PC's index. Never allocate on reset,\n  bubbles, illegal encodings, data-memory holds or active divide waits.\n  Only conditional branches train the agree counters. Use read-before-\n  write lookup with no same-cycle update-to-prediction bypass.\n\n  Add a full-width predicted_target to if_id_t and id_ex_t while retaining\n  predicted_taken. Capture the exact selected target together with its\n  instruction in ID/EX's existing data domain on \"'!stall || flush. Hold it\n  on true stalls; no reset is needed for this data because existing valid\n  and predicted_taken clearing make bubble contents inert. Prediction\n  metadata and PC advancement must use the same live lookup at acceptance.\n\n  Keep authoritative branch/jump resolution unchanged. EX recovery is\n  in.valid && ex_advance && ((actual_taken ''^ in.predicted_taken) ||\n  (actual_taken && in.predicted_taken &&\n   resolved_pc_next != in.predicted_target)). The target comparison is\n  essential for changing JALR destinations and stale taken BTB entries.\n  An ordinary or illegal word reached through an old entry recovers to\n  its architectural sequential successor. Changed direct targets, tag\n  conflicts, reserved encodings and target faults must retain full\n  correctness. Do not assume that a matching tag validates an instruction.\n\n  Preserve immediate recovery with its existing one-slot penalty. Reset\n  has highest PC priority, then accepted EX recovery, then ordinary fetch\n  acceptance. Recovery overrides imem backpressure on data-memory release\n  and squashes younger side effects at the existing edge. Keep all real\n  and ALTOPS arithmetic, resolved operand registers, effective-address\n  transport, memory handshakes, link writes, trap suppression and nret=1.\n  RVFI always reports authoritative resolved next-PC and contiguous order.\n  No evaluation-contract files or correctness gates change.\n\n  A temporary prototype of exactly this 32-entry distributed design\n  measured placement frequencies 79.411, 82.122 and 82.122 MHz, for median\n  82.122 MHz. Option zero used 4,311 logic cells, 873 registers, 70 LUT RAMs,\n  four BSRAMs and two DSPs. Canonical stalled CoreMark passed CRC, UART,\n  memory-bounds, completion and timing-marker validation at 4,247,943\n  cycles for ten iterations, versus 4,239,051 for the champion. The product\n  is approximately 193.322 iterations/sec, about 1.00 percent above 191.41.\n  This measured gain does NOT clear the 4.6 percent acceptance margin:\n  at this cycle count the candidate needs more than about 85.050 MHz.\n  Expected impact is therefore one percent with low confidence in\n  acceptance, not an unsupported above-threshold claim. Divider recurrence,\n  operand bypass, recovery fanout and cache routing remain timing risks.\n\n  The target-cache prototype passed full-field RVFI cosimulation on\n  selftest and random1/random2/random3, each with ready buses and with\n  both buses stalled, plus all 53 unchanged standard ALTOPS formal checks.\n  The distributed-memory attribute changes implementation, not logical\n  behavior. Strict Verilator lint passed with narrowly scoped unused-low-\n  bit annotations on the full-width BTB update address inputs. These are\n  proposal-stage measurements, not tournament acceptance. Rerun all\n  unchanged correctness gates and scored placements after implementation.\n  Restore temporary prototype RTL and tests; this YAML is the deliverable.\n\n  Extend independent predictor tests for cold misses, both halves of the\n  32-entry table, full-tag conflicts, reset, read-before-write updates and\n  acceptance after arbitrary holds. Add changing-target JALR, returns to\n  multiple callers, changed instruction words, taken-to-taken wrong-target\n  recovery, illegal and misaligned targets, imem-stalled recovery and\n  data-memory release. Check exact RVFI PC/link/trap/order, once-only\n  training and no wrong-path stores or divide launches. Update old\n  predictor-specific scoreboards for cold misses without weakening their\n  architectural assertions, progress bounds or memory-handshake checks.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 1\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the 32-entry full-PC-tagged distributed-RAM target cache and\n      existing-counter direction selection; predict JAL/JALR from saved\n      targets, use PC+4 on misses, and remove instruction-derived target\n      arithmetic from next-PC. Reset validity only and expose target metadata.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add predicted_target before instr in both IF/ID and ID/EX, preserving\n      the low predicted_taken/valid fields and architectural pc_next payloads.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture the exact predicted target in the data domain with its accepted\n      instruction, retain it on stalls, and preserve existing control squash.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add target-mismatch recovery and once-only aligned branch/JAL/JALR\n      target-cache updates. Preserve authoritative target, link and fault\n      handling, conditional agreement training and all execution timing.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire full PC, target, kind and valid update feedback from EX to IF;\n      preserve external ports, hazard priorities, forwarding and RVFI wiring.\n  - file: test/test_if_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Model tagged target lookup, distributed-table banks, agreement training,\n      cold/conflict misses, reset and acceptance-edge read-before-write behavior.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt prediction-only scoreboards and packed metadata offsets while\n      preserving correctness checks. Add changing indirect targets, stale\n      target/word recovery, alignment faults, holds and wrong-path suppression.\n'\"''')\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nobj=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(obj,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Schema valid. Fitness:',82.122e6*10/4247943,'Acceptance MHz:',191.41*1.046*4247943/10/1e6)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
