{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_30", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"tail -8 cores/bench/generated/r15s2_probe3_timing.log; sed -n '1,150p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r15s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261004-003-r15s2\ntitle: \"Pipeline predictor recovery after removing the arithmetic timing bottleneck\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion reaches 202.72 iterations/sec at 85.936 MHz and\n  4,239,051 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path now starts\n  at the resolved EX branch operands and ends at the fetch PC. Earlier\n  registered-recovery experiment r11s2 added about 1.6 percent cycles but\n  remained limited by arithmetic that has since been substantially\n  shortened. The r13s2/r14s2 target buffers introduced cold/conflict misses\n  and routing costs. Revisit recovery pipelining on this changed critical\n  path while retaining the successful sixteen-entry agreement predictor.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert one register boundary in the predictor's EX-to-fetch recovery\n  feedback. Keep the existing instruction-derived direct targets, direct\n  JAL prediction, sixteen local agreement counters and direction-only\n  mismatch test. EX computes its authoritative resolved next PC and a\n  valid recovery event exactly once when a valid instruction advances.\n  Capture that target and event in dedicated flops. Fetch redirects from\n  the registered event on the following edge, with reset then recovery\n  then ordinary accepted prediction as PC priorities. Keep io_imemAddr\n  driven directly by the fetch PC; do not put a combinational correction\n  mux before instruction memory or introduce a target cache.\n\n  Defer the resolving instruction's passage to MEM by exactly one cycle.\n  At miss detection capture its EX/MEM data and metadata, save its resolved\n  controls, and expose an inert bubble rather than a valid MEM instruction.\n  Hold that captured payload during the recovery-pending cycle, then\n  activate the saved controls and validity at the PC-repair edge. This\n  preserves the existing formal ten-clock progress bound when the correct\n  target is a nine-clock divide; never relax that bound. During the cycle\n  in which recovery is pending, the younger instruction which may have\n  entered ID/EX mus\n…[3443 of 6515 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:00b5a5e6ac8ea9fd]…\nreserve all top-level\n      ports, nret=1 RVFI wiring and existing datapath interfaces.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Give pending registered recovery priority over younger operand and\n      instruction-bus holds for ID/EX squash; preserve real older dmem\n      holds and retirement. Ensure a killed EX divide cannot delay recovery.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Consume only the registered correction event and target, preserving\n      direct PC-to-imem wiring, accepted-fetch prediction, local counter\n      training and reset/recovery/normal-PC priority.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add directed taken/not-taken misses with wrong-path stores, dependent\n      writers, branches and divides. Exercise imem stalls, dmem-release\n      recovery and trap cases. Verify exact architectural PC/link/trap and\n      contiguous RVFI order, no wrong-path side effects or training, one\n      correction per advancing miss, progress and the two-slot miss cost.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
