{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_32", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261004-003-r15s2.yaml')\ndata={\n'id':'hyp-20261004-003-r15s2',\n'title':'Preselect predictor correction targets and isolate branch outcomes from fetch holds',\n'category':'predictor',\n'motivation':'''The current champion scores 202.72 iterations/sec at 85.936 MHz and 4,239,051 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its worst timing path starts at a resolved EX branch operand and ends at the fetch PC enable. The detailed vendor report exposes an indirect path through trap-qualified writer eligibility and decode hazards, in addition to the intended recovery path. Earlier r7s2 correction-offset preselection did not remove that dependency and was still limited by arithmetic; r13s2/r14s2 target buffers introduced cold/conflict misses. Optimize the existing direction-only predictor's correction network without changing its predictions or miss penalty.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Keep the sixteen-entry agreement predictor, instruction-derived direct targets, direct-JAL prediction and immediate one-slot recovery. Restructure only EX's predictor-recovery fanout so the late branch outcome determines whether to redirect, while the saved prediction independently selects the correction address and conditional branch faults cannot affect fetch holds through forwarding eligibility.\n\nIn rtl/ex_stage.sv retain the current actual_taken, resolved_pc_next, ex_advance and direction-mismatch redirect predicate. Replace only the correction-address assignment with:\n  redirect_target = in.predicted_taken ? (in.pc + 32'd4)\n                  : (in.ctrl.is_jump ? jump_target : branch_target);\nAll operands of this address selection come from the already registered instruction, PC, immediate, resolved rs1 and prediction. Keep the full-width PC+4, branch target and jump target computations available in parallel; do not preselect an offset before a shared adder. The correction address is meaningful only when redirect is asserted. On a miss, actual_taken is the opposite of the saved predicted_taken: a predicted-taken miss always corrects to PC+4, and a predicted-not-taken miss always corrects to the resolved branch/jump target. Thus branch_cond no longer drives the full-width recovery-address selection. JALR retains its exact resolved rs1+immediate target with bit zero cleared.\n\nRemove the other branch-outcome-to-fetch-control dependency by defining:\n  next_reg_write = in.valid && in.ctrl.reg_write\n                && \"'!in.ctrl.is_illegal && !misalign_jump;\nUse the original registered decoder controls here, not ctrl_with_trap. The validated decoder never sets reg_write for a conditional branch, so a conditional branch'\"'s outcome-dependent alignment fault cannot suppress a real writer and must not enter the EX forwarding producer predicate. A faulting JAL/JALR still has misalign_jump asserted and must not forward or write its link. Keep ctrl_with_trap unchanged for the architectural EX/MEM trap payload, including conditional branch faults and link suppression. Do not replace the architectural resolved_pc_next with the speculative correction candidate: RVFI must continue to report the actual successor even on correctly predicted transfers or target faults.\n\nRetain all existing advance qualification, once-only agreement training, prediction capture, bus handshakes, recovery-over-imem-stall priority, data-memory holds, load/M interlocks, arithmetic latency and ALTOPS behavior. Neither recovery nor training may repeat while EX is held. Preserve nret=1, decoder legality, contiguous retirement order, target alignment semantics and the unchanged formal progress bound. This adds no predictor state, target cache, stage or extra recovery cycle.\n\nA temporary prototype consisting of exactly these two EX assignment changes passed strict Verilator lint and canonical stalled CoreMark validation at the unchanged 4,239,051 bracketed cycles for ten iterations. Gowin placement options measured 87.863, 90.699 and 90.699 MHz, yielding a 90.699 MHz median and approximately 213.96 iterations/sec: 5.54 percent above the 202.72 champion. Option zero used 4,393 logic cells, 951 registers, 40 LUT RAMs, four BSRAMs and two DSPs. These are proposal-stage measurements, not tournament acceptance. The measured gain exceeds the 4.6 percent threshold, but with less than one percentage point of headroom; placement sensitivity and the remaining arithmetic/recovery-squash paths justify medium confidence. Reproduce the exact two-assignment topology and rerun all unchanged correctness gates and scored placements after implementation.\n\nUse existing predictor scoreboards plus directed regression cases to check both correction directions, learned forward-taken and backward-not-taken branches, dependent JALR links, target alignment faults, full-width target wraparound, and recovery coincident with instruction stalls or older memory release. Check redirect_target against the authoritative successor only when redirect is valid, and always check RVFI pc_wdata against the architectural successor. Explicitly verify that a misaligned taken conditional branch traps without appearing as a register producer, and that a misaligned JALR cannot forward or write a link. Require no wrong-path store/divider launch and unchanged one-slot recovery and retirement-gap bounds. Restore temporary prototype files after research; this hypothesis YAML is the deliverable.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Select the correction target from the saved predicted_taken bit, using parallel PC+4 and resolved branch/jump targets, while retaining authoritative resolved_pc_next for RVFI. Define EX writer eligibility from registered valid/legal/reg_write controls and misalign_jump only, removing conditional branch outcome from forwarding-induced fetch holds. Keep the existing redirect predicate, architectural trap payload and predictor training unchanged.'''\n },\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Preserve existing predictor and architectural assertions. Add focused recovery cases for both direction errors, branch versus JALR alignment faults, dependent link use, imem-stalled/dmem-release recovery, and wrong-path stores/divides. Check the correction address only when redirect is asserted and retain exact resolved RVFI PC/trap/link values, once-only training, one-slot miss behavior and the unchanged progress bound.'''}]\n}\nclass Dumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef represent_str(dumper, value):\n return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',value,style='|' if '\\\\n' in value or len(value)>100 else None)\nDumper.add_representer(str,represent_str)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(data,Dumper=Dumper,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Written and schema-valid:',p)\nprint('Probe fitness',90.699e6*10/4239051,'gain',100*(90.699e6*10/4239051/202.72-1))\nPY\npytest -q cores/bench/test > cores/bench/generated/r15s2_final_tests.log 2>&1\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
